# How this compares to other private-AI setups

> **Historical (gateway era).** Written for the LiteLLM + Ollama stack; superseded by ADR 0016. See `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` for the current design.

Read 2026-08-02. Two reference points worth knowing about: one person solving
the same problem from a different angle, and one product solving the piece we
found most annoying.

---

## Vitalik Buterin — "My self-sovereign / local / private / secure LLM setup"

[April 2026](https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2026/04/02/secure_llms.html).
Near-identical brief: *"what kind of AI setup would we build if we took privacy,
security and self-sovereignty as non-negotiable?"*

### Where we independently landed on the same thing

- **SearXNG** for search, for the same reason — reduce what any single engine
  learns about you.
- **AGENTS.md** as the shared instruction file.
- **Local-first with a deliberate cloud escape hatch.** He hit the same wall we
  did: local models are fine on well-trodden ground and fall apart on hard
  problems. He tried and failed to get Qwen3.5:35B to implement BLS-12-381
  hash-to-point, and sent it to Claude, which one-shotted it. Our `cloud-code`
  role exists for exactly that moment.
- **Roughly the same speed floor.** He says "anything slower than 50 tok/sec
  feels too annoying to be worth it." Our roles run 65–120.

### Where he is ahead — and these are real gaps for us

**1. Sandboxing — closed, after this was written.**

He runs LLM work inside [bubblewrap](https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap):
`sbox` in any directory creates a sandbox rooted there, and anything started
inside can only see that directory plus explicit whitelists. Ports, audio and
filesystem are all constrained.

We had nothing equivalent when this file was first written: `aider` and Zed's
agent ran with full user permissions, able to read `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, every
repo on the machine, and the gateway's own `.env`. `scripts/sandboxed.sh` and
ADR 0007 answered that — an SBPL profile that restricts capability rather than
attempting to detect injection. Different mechanism from bubblewrap, same
instinct.

**2. A threat model we never wrote down: prompt injection.**

He cites research that roughly **15% of published agent skills contained
malicious instructions**, and a demonstration where summarising a web page
caused an agent to download and execute a shell script. Our stack has SearXNG
wired into a chat UI and agents that read repositories. Both are injection
surfaces. We have thought hard about where data *goes* and not at all about what
untrusted content might *make the agent do*.

**3. Human confirmation as a firewall.**

His messaging daemon can read messages and send only to himself autonomously.
Sending to anyone else requires explicit confirmation. His framing is the one
worth stealing:

> the new "two-factor confirmation" is that the two factors are the human and
> the LLM ... the hope is that humans and LLMs fail in distinct ways

We have `--no-auto-commits` and Zed's tool-restricted profiles, which are the
same instinct applied only to code. Nothing gates an action with consequences.

**4. A local `world_knowledge` corpus.**

A Wikipedia dump plus manuals for things he cares about, so questions answerable
locally never reach a search engine. For a regulated domain that would be
reference docs and internal playbooks — the queries most worth not leaking. Our `local-embed` role
exists and indexes nothing.

### Where we are ahead

**Multi-machine access.** He dismisses the DGX Spark partly because *"you have to
figure out the networking details of how to connect to it from your actual work
device"*. That is precisely what our gateway plus scoped keys plus client shim
solves. His setup is one-machine; ours serves laptops with per-device keys,
spend caps and revocation.

**Structural privacy at a different layer.** His isolation is process-level
(sandbox). Ours is route-level (key scope). They fail differently and are
complementary rather than competing:

| Threat | Sandbox | Key scope |
|---|---|---|
| Agent reads a file it shouldn't | **caught** | not caught |
| Prompt sends client data to a cloud model | not caught | **caught** |
| Compromised client machine | partly | **caught** — key is scoped |
| Malicious skill exfiltrates via curl | **caught** | not caught |

We have one column. He has the other. Having both is the correct answer.

**Measurement.** He reports tok/s per hardware. We bench roles on real tasks and
record the decision. His post is explicitly a starting point, not a finished
product — he says so in a warning at the top.

**Model indirection.** He swaps models by hand through llama-swap; we address
roles, so nothing downstream changes.

### One tooling disagreement worth noting

He moved from Ollama to `llama-server` + `llama-swap`, because **Ollama could
not fit Qwen3.5:35B on his GPU and llama-server could**. That is a real
efficiency difference, not tribal preference. On 64 GB unified we are not
memory-pressed at 37.5 GB resident, so it has not bitten us — but if a future
role does not fit, that is the first thing to try before buying hardware.

---

## Routerly — the database-free gateway

[github.com/Inebrio/Routerly](https://github.com/Inebrio/Routerly), AGPL-3.0.
Directly addresses the thing we found most irritating.

### What it does that LiteLLM does not

| | Routerly | LiteLLM (ours) |
|---|---|---|
| Database | **none** — JSON in `~/.routerly/` | Postgres + Prisma, 64 tables |
| Native Anthropic `/v1/messages` | **yes** | no — translates to OpenAI format |
| Per-project tokens + budgets | yes | yes (virtual keys) |
| Per-project model list | yes | yes |
| Routing policies | 9, including LLM-picks-the-model | static fallbacks |
| Provider breadth | 8 | 100+ |
| MCP gateway | **none** | yes — scoped by key, team, org |
| Maturity | v0.1.5, 43 stars, 3 contributors | widely deployed |

**Checked again 2026-08-02: Routerly still has no MCP support.** That now cuts
against switching. LiteLLM's MCP gateway applies the same key scoping to tools
as to models, which is what ADR 0008 is built on — leaving would mean rebuilding
tool access control as well as the routing verification below.

**The interesting unlock is native Anthropic format.** Claude Code is currently a
documented exception in our stack because LiteLLM only speaks OpenAI shape.
Routerly handles `/v1/messages` natively, so Claude Code could route through the
gateway and gain spend tracking and logging. That is a real capability, not a
tidiness argument.

### Why we are not switching yet

- **v0.1.5, one release, three contributors, 43 stars.** This is pre-1.0
  software that would hold our provider keys and enforce our privacy boundary.
  LiteLLM's 64 tables are ugly; ugly is not the same as risky.
- **The cost of switching is the verification, not the config.** The leak test,
  fallback audit, digest pinning and every script touching `/key/*` would need
  redoing and re-proving. That is the expensive part, and it buys removing
  something that currently runs in a container we never look at.
- **AGPL plus a commercial-licensing address** is an open-core shape. Worth
  watching whether the free tier narrows.

Revisit at 1.0, or the first time Postgres actually causes a problem rather
than merely offending.

---

## What we should actually do about this

In rough priority. Items 1 and 2 are now done; the rest stand.

1. ~~**Sandboxing for agent work.**~~ Done — `scripts/sandboxed.sh`, ADR 0007.
2. ~~**Write down a prompt-injection threat model.**~~ Partly done. ADR 0007
   covers agent containment and ADR 0008 covers tool scope. Web search feeding
   the chat UI is still unmodelled.
3. **Confirmation gates for consequential actions.** Currently only `git commit`
   qualifies, which is why this has not hurt. It would matter the moment
   anything in this stack can send an email or move money.
4. **`world_knowledge` corpus.** Domain reference material and internal
   playbooks indexed locally via `local-embed`, so the queries most worth not
   leaking never reach SearXNG.
5. **Note llama-server** as the first thing to try if a model does not fit,
   before assuming the hardware is the limit.

The honest summary as first written: **we built the routing and governance
layer well, and the isolation layer not at all.** Vitalik built the isolation
layer and skipped the routing. ADR 0007 closed most of our side of that gap,
and ADR 0008 extended the governance layer to cover tools as well as models —
which was a hole neither of us had named.
# What we have tried

A running record of serving-stack and role experiments: what was tried, what
it measured, and why it stayed or went. Newest last. Add a row whenever an
ADR lands or a bench run changes a decision.

Model names stay out of this file (hard rule 1) — which weights each role
used is in `docs/MODELS.md` (Live / Retired). Raw numbers are in
`bench/results/<run>/`, committed since 2026-10-31. Runs before that were
re-checked in and had domain wording in old task prompts and model answers
redacted (billing/invoice substituted); figures are unchanged.

## Serving stack

| Date | Tried | Result | Outcome | Record |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-08-01 | Inherited setup: Ollama + LiteLLM, ad-hoc model names | Worked, no role discipline, no privacy boundary | Rebuilt around roles and scoped keys | ADR 0001 |
| 2026-08-02 | Postgres + Prisma for LiteLLM virtual keys | Per-key scope; leak test passes | Kept until 0016 | ADR 0002 |
| 2026-08-02 | Ollama bound to localhost; LAN via gateway only | LAN probe unreachable | Kept until 0016 | ADR 0003 |
| 2026-08-02 | Agents under `sandboxed.sh`, egress only to the gateway | Probe proves nothing else reachable | Kept; target port moves to `:1234` | ADR 0007 |
| 2026-08-02 | MCP tools through the gateway, deny by default, scoped by key | Works; adds gateway coupling | Superseded by 0016 (MCP moves to the client) | ADR 0008 |
| 2026-09-04 | Context capped at 64K for local roles | Fits the RAM envelope | Kept | ADR 0010 |
| 2026-09-04 | Ollama memory knobs: flash attention, q8_0 KV, 1 loaded model, 1 parallel | One slot for every client; any role switch evicts the agent model | Superseded by 0016 (F1, F2) | ADR 0011 |
| 2026-09-06 | `local-big` on Rapid-MLX `:8000` | Tool smoke 2.6 s vs 12–18 s on Ollama | Superseded by 0013 (second server to supervise) | ADR 0012, `2026-09-06-1229-tool-smoke` |
| 2026-10-22 | `local-big` on Ollama's MLX runner, one inference server | ~111 tok/s; Zed agent loops still stall under load | Superseded by 0016 | ADR 0013, `2026-10-22-0816` |
| 2026-10-31 | Stall handling: 90 s timeout, no retries, `mlx-watchdog` kills a stuck runner | Treats the symptom (F7) | Superseded by 0016 | ADR 0015 |
| 2026-10-31 | Phase 0 concurrency baseline on that stack | c1 TTFT p95 21.0 s; c3 p95 65.8 s, strictly serialised; no failures, peak 37 GB | Numbers to beat | `2026-10-31-phase0` |
| 2026-10-31 | D6 switched to the LM Studio app owning the server | llmster and the app cannot share `~/.lmstudio`; app costs ~1.1 GB | Kept | ADR 0016 |
| 2026-10-31 | Podman VM died after login | launchd kills a job's leftover processes when it exits; reproduced with a test job | `AbandonProcessGroup` on `com.hearth.boot`; VM survives the job | — |
| 2026-10-31 | Phase 4 as an SSH tunnel with tunnel-only keys (D8), not LAN serving | Host unexposed; tested against a throwaway sshd: `restrict` alone still ran remote commands, a forced command fixed it | Kept; laptop gate pending | ADR 0016, `docs/LAPTOP.md` |
| 2026-10-31 | Zed picker labels generated from roles.tsv (`zed-sync.sh`) | Clients still call `local-big`; label cannot go stale | Kept; rule 1 amended for generated labels | — |
| 2026-10-31 | Phase 5 scripts against LM Studio; weight hashing (D4); login agent | healthcheck 10 pass / 1 warn, preflight 29 pass; negative controls fail as designed; found saved settings drifted to 0.0.0.0 + CORS, and unknown model names served by local-big | Kept | ADR 0016 |
| 2026-10-31 | Old stack deleted without a soak | Ports 4000/5433/11434 free; tool-smoke and sandbox-probe still pass | No rollback; backups in `~/hearth-backups/` | ADR 0016 |
| 2026-10-31 | Phase 1: llmster direct, `local-big` 3 parallel × 64K | c3 TTFT p95 59.9 s (was 65.8); prefill still serialised, all three finish at ~61 s; peak 29.7 GB; 3/40 wrong-tool picks | Accepted by operator; no further bench | ADR 0016, `2026-10-31-phase1` |
| 2026-10-31 | `local-big` context raised to 128K, still 3 slots | Model card asks for ≥128K so thinking holds. Reload honored 131072 (not 262144). Idle footprint 20.7 GB; c3 bench not re-run | Kept | ADR 0017 |
| 2026-11-01 | Draft MTP on for `local-big` | Same weights. Speeds decode, not the prefill wait. `lms load` accepted the flag; the server log does not name the drafter, and `lms ps` cannot show it | Flag set | — |
| 2026-11-01 | Drop `local-embed` | Nothing requested embeddings except healthcheck. Weights stay on disk, unloaded | Removed | ADR 0018 |

## Roles

| Date | Tried | Result | Outcome | Record |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-08-02 | 70B dense model as `local-big` | 42 GB, could not stay resident; 440 s to first reply | Retired | ADR 0004 |
| 2026-08-02 | 30B MoE as `local-big` / `local-code` | Fits beside other roles; fast | `local-code` kept; `local-big` reassigned | ADR 0004, 0005 |
| 2026-08-02 | Separate `local-fast` small coder | MoE already generates at small-model speed | Role retired | ADR 0005 |
| 2026-08-02 | `local-chat` small general model, thinking on | Very verbose: 7–11× the tokens of the coder | Thinking disabled: 29.7 s → 5.1 s | ADR 0005, 0006, `2026-08-02-*` |
| 2026-09-04 | 27B dense MTP coding model as candidate | 19 tok/s, 14× tokens | Not adopted | `2026-09-04-1948` |
| 2026-09-06 | 20B reasoning model as `local-big` | Slower tool smoke than the MLX agent model | Retired | ADR 0012 |
| 2026-10-22 | `cloud-cheap` on a newer Flash tier | Thinks by default; tiny caps return empty | Kept, reasoning advertised | ADR 0014 |
| 2026-10-31 | Collapse `local-code` / `local-chat` into `local-big` (D1) | Decided; avoids evicting the agent model | Phase 1 onward | ADR 0016 |
# Editors: Zed and aider

Two tools, deliberately. They are good at different halves of the work and both
address roles rather than model names, so neither needs touching when a model
changes.

| | aider | Zed |
|---|---|---|
| Where | terminal | editor |
| Unit of work | a diff you approve | inline edits and agent runs |
| Best for | changes you want to review before they land | exploring, refactoring in place |
| Agent | n/a — it always shows you the diff | yes, with restrictable tools |

---

## aider

### Config

```yaml
# ~/.aider.conf.yml
# Roles, not model names. OPENAI_API_BASE + OPENAI_API_KEY come from
# scripts/aliases.zsh per invocation. One local model (ADR 0016, D1).
model: openai/local-big
weak-model: openai/local-big
dark-mode: true
auto-commits: true
map-tokens: 4096
```

`weak-model` is used for commit messages and summaries. With one resident
model there is nothing cheaper to point it at, and nothing to evict.
`ask-cloud` overrides both with the Gemini id from `config/roles.tsv`, so a
cloud session never sends its commit messages anywhere else.

### Architect mode

aider can split planning from editing. With one local model both sides are
`local-big`; the value left is that the design step stays visible.

```bash
ask-arch    # aider --architect --model openai/local-big --editor-model openai/local-big
```

### Switching mid-session

```
/model openai/local-big       the only local role
                              (cloud: quit and start `ask-cloud` instead —
                               it uses its own key and endpoint)
/ask                          question without editing anything
/architect                    toggle architect mode
```

`/ask` is worth the habit — it answers without proposing a diff, which keeps you
deciding what to change.

### Deliberate review

You have `auto-commits: true`, which is convenient and also the setting most
likely to let a change land without you reading it. For anything touching
flagged sensitive code:

```bash
aider --no-auto-commits
```

---

## Zed

### Point it at LM Studio

Directly in `~/.config/zed/settings.json` (ADR 0016). Every model slot —
agent, inline assistant, commit message, thread summary, compaction,
subagent — is `hearth/local-big`, so nothing ever asks for a second model.
**No cloud provider on this host (D3)**: cloud is `ask-cloud` in a terminal.

```json
{
  "agent": {
    "default_model":          { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" },
    "inline_assistant_model": { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" },
    "commit_message_model":   { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" },
    "thread_summary_model":   { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" },
    "compaction_model":       { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" },
    "subagent_model":         { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" }
  },
  "language_models": {
    "openai_compatible": {
      "hearth": {
        "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "available_models": [
          { "name": "local-big", "display_name": "<written by scripts/zed-sync.sh>", "max_tokens": 131072 }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`name` is the role and is what Zed sends. `display_name` and `max_tokens`
are generated by `scripts/zed-sync.sh` from `config/roles.tsv` + `lms ls`
(e.g. `local-big · <model> <quant>`), and `scripts/lms-load.sh` re-runs it
after every load, so the picker always shows what is actually loaded and the
window always matches the load context. Preflight warns if they drift. Add a
new role's entry by hand once; the sync keeps it current.

The token goes in through the UI (Agent panel → settings → `hearth` → API
key) so it lands in the keychain rather than a dotfile. Use the host token on
this machine; each laptop gets its own (phase 4).

### MCP: auslaw, Research profile only

`auslaw` is a `context_servers` stdio entry (same seven tools, no jade
cookie — `docs/MCP.md`). Every one of its useful tools sends the query to
AustLII, so it is enabled **only** in the `research` agent profile.

Zed's built-in **Write** profile sets `enable_all_context_servers: true`
(Zed 1.26 defaults), which would expose auslaw in every thread. Settings
redefine Write with that set to `false`. A profile override **replaces** the
built-in profile (`name` is required, tools are not merged), so the override
carries Zed 1.26.2's full Write tool list. Re-check it after a Zed upgrade:
a partial override fails the whole settings load with `missing field name`. `tool_permissions.default` is `confirm`, so each
tool call asks first.

Switching to Research is the act of saying "this query goes to AustLII" —
the same thing switching to the `research` key used to mean.

Note that Write and Ask still include Zed's built-in `fetch` and
`search_web` tools, which reach the internet on their own. That predates
this migration.

### On a laptop

Through an SSH tunnel to the host — `docs/LAPTOP.md`. Zed points at
`http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` exactly as on the host (the tunnel's local end),
with the **laptop's own** token. Run `ai-tunnel` before opening Zed. A Gemini
provider is allowed on the laptop (D3).

Zed's SSH remote projects also work: edit files on the host over SSH while
the model call goes through the tunnel. That needs a normal SSH key, not the
tunnel-only one.

### Agent profiles — the reason Zed is here

Zed's agent has **profiles with configurable tool access**. You can define one
that reads and proposes but cannot apply edits or run commands, and a separate
one that can.

That is the closest thing shipping today to an assistant that helps without
taking over, and it is why Zed earns its place over JetBrains here — JetBrains
only supports chat on local models.

Suggested split:

- **explore** — read files, search, discuss. No edits, no terminal. Use when you
  are trying to understand something.
- **build** — full tools. Use when you already know what you want.

The distinction is not about safety. It is about which mode you are in.

---

## Why two tools, and why this matters

The 2026 research on AI-assisted development is not comfortable reading.
Developers using AI assistance scored **17% lower on comprehension** afterwards,
with the steepest decline among those who fully delegated (Shen and Tamkin,
2026). The framing that stuck is **output up, capability down** — and the
distinction between *atrophy*, losing skills you had, and *foreclosure*, never
building them. Roughly half of programmer actions in LLM-assisted workflows show
identifiable cognitive bias, mostly anchoring on whatever the model proposed
first (Zhou et al., 2026).

Nothing ships to address this. The one paper directly on it —
[Agents That Teach](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.06101) — is a design proposal,
not a product. The measurement is years ahead of the tooling.

So the mitigations here are choices, not features:

- **aider shows a diff and waits.** That is friction, and the friction is the
  point. `/ask` and `--no-auto-commits` add more when you want it.
- **Zed's explore profile can't edit.** Reading and discussing without the option
  to apply keeps you deciding.
- **Local models are weaker, and that is quietly protective.** A model that
  cannot one-shot the answer keeps you in the loop — the opposite of how model
  quality is usually discussed.
- **Architect mode separates deciding from typing**, which keeps the design
  decision visible instead of buried in a diff.

None of this is about distrusting the output. It is about noticing when you have
stopped thinking, which by definition is not something you notice.

---

## Other editors

Not recommended here, recorded so the question does not come round again.

| Editor | State |
|---|---|
| JetBrains | Native LiteLLM support, free tier, but **chat only** on local models — no agent, no next-edit |
| VS Code + Continue | Works fully, but an extension rather than built in |
| Cursor | Not used here — rejects non-HTTPS base URLs; agent mode unreliable with custom endpoints |
# Laptop access

ADR 0016, phase 4 decision: **SSH tunnel only.** The host's LM Studio stays
bound to `127.0.0.1:1234`; a laptop forwards a local port to it over SSH. Same
setup on the LAN and away from it, traffic encrypted, nothing on the host
exposed to the network.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph laptop["Laptop"]
        lz["Zed / aider / ai-*"]
        lp["127.0.0.1:1234<br/>127.0.0.1:3000"]
    end
    subgraph host["Host"]
        sshd["sshd :22<br/>tunnel-only key"]
        lms["LM Studio 127.0.0.1:1234<br/>token per machine"]
        webui["Open WebUI 127.0.0.1:3000"]
    end
    gem["Gemini<br/>laptop's own key"]
    lz --> lp
    lp -->|"ssh -L"| sshd
    sshd --> lms
    sshd --> webui
    lz -.->|"ask-cloud only"| gem
```

## What each laptop gets

| Thing | Where | Why |
|---|---|---|
| LM Studio token `laptop-<name>` | laptop `~/.hearth-key` (mode 600) | revoke one machine without touching the others |
| SSH key `hearth_tunnel` | laptop `~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel`; host `authorized_keys` with `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen=…,permitlisten="none:1"` | the key can `-L` to `127.0.0.1:1234` and `:3000` only — no shell, no commands, no `-R`, no other ports |
| Google API key (optional) | laptop `~/.hearth-gemini-key` (mode 600) | `ask-cloud` with the laptop's own key; the host's key never leaves |
| `client/hearth-client.sh` / `.ps1` | laptop `~/.hearth-client.sh` | `ai-tunnel`, `ai-ping`, `ai-models`, `ai-ask`, `ai-webui`, `ask*`, `ask-cloud` |

Secrets are read from files per command and never exported into the
laptop's shell. A world-readable key file is refused.

## Setup

On the host, `scripts/client-setup.sh <laptop-name>` prints every step with
this host's name and user filled in (`--windows` for PowerShell). In short:

1. **Host, LM Studio app:** Developer → Server Settings → Manage Tokens →
   create `laptop-<name>`. Leave Serve on Local Network **off**.
2. **Laptop:** `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel`, and a `Host hearth`
   entry in `~/.ssh/config` pointing at the host with `IdentitiesOnly yes`.
3. **Host:** `scripts/client-setup.sh <name> --authorize hearth_tunnel.pub`.
   It refuses a key that is already authorized without restrictions.
4. **Laptop:** copy `client/hearth-client.sh` to `~/.hearth-client.sh`,
   store the token and (optionally) a Google key, add
   `HEARTH_SSH=hearth`, `HEARTH_CLOUD_MODEL=…` and `source ~/.hearth-client.sh`
   to the shell rc.
5. **Laptop:** `ai-tunnel && ai-ping && ai-models`.

`ai-ping` answers: `up`, `BAD TOKEN` (revoked or wrong token), `NO ANSWER`
(tunnel up but host asleep or LM Studio down — on the host, `scripts/boot.sh`),
or `DOWN` (no tunnel — `ai-tunnel`).

## AI-assisted setup

For an AI assistant (Claude Code, Cursor, …) running **on the laptop** in a
clone of this repo. The assistant is probably a cloud model: anything it reads
or prints goes to its vendor. That decides every rule below.

**Never:** read, print, grep, copy or edit `~/.hearth-key`,
`~/.hearth-gemini-key` or `~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel` (the private key); ask the
person to paste a token or key into the chat; run anything in `scripts/`
(host-only); add a router port-forward; suggest turning on Serve on Local
Network; configure hearth as a Cursor model provider (see below). Checking a
secret file's permissions with `ls -l` is fine.

**1. Ask the person for:**

- the host as `user@host.local` (on the host: `id -un` and
  `scutil --get LocalHostName`);
- the laptop's name for its token and key comment (letters, digits, `-`, `_`);
- shell: macOS / Linux (zsh or bash) or Windows PowerShell.

Read the Gemini model id yourself: the `cloud-cheap` row of
`config/roles.tsv`, column 3. Do not copy it anywhere except the
`HEARTH_CLOUD_MODEL` line.

**2. You may run** (macOS / Linux; Windows equivalents are in
`client/hearth-client.ps1`'s header):

```bash
# Interactive (passphrase prompt) — ask the person to run it, or run it if your
# terminal is interactive:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel -C hearth-tunnel-<name>

# ~/.ssh/config — append only if there is no "Host hearth" entry already
printf 'Host hearth\n  HostName %s\n  User %s\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel\n  IdentitiesOnly yes\n' <host.local> <user> >> ~/.ssh/config

cp client/hearth-client.sh ~/.hearth-client.sh
printf 'export HEARTH_SSH=hearth\nexport HEARTH_CLOUD_MODEL=%s\nsource ~/.hearth-client.sh\n' <cloud-cheap id> >> ~/.zshrc   # or ~/.bashrc
```

Showing the person `~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel.pub` is fine — it is public.

**3. Stop and hand over to the person** for each of these, then wait:

1. *On the host*, LM Studio app → Developer → Server Settings → Manage Tokens →
   create `laptop-<name>`.
2. *On the host*, with the `.pub` file copied over:
   `scripts/client-setup.sh <name> --authorize hearth_tunnel.pub`.
3. *On the laptop, in their own terminal* (not through you), store the secrets:

   ```bash
   umask 077
   read -rs t && printf '%s' "$t" > ~/.hearth-key && unset t          # laptop-<name> token
   read -rs t && printf '%s' "$t" > ~/.hearth-gemini-key && unset t   # optional: this laptop's OWN Google key
   ```

Then confirm with `ls -l ~/.hearth-key ~/.hearth-gemini-key`: mode
`-rw-------`, non-zero size. Nothing more.

**4. Prove it** in a new shell. Both halves are required: the first shows the
key works, the second that it can do nothing else.

| Command | Expect |
|---|---|
| `ai-tunnel` | `tunnel: up` (the first run asks the person to confirm the host fingerprint) |
| `ai-ping` | `up` |
| `ai-models` | `local-big` |
| `ai-ask "say ok"` | a short reply from local-big |
| `ssh hearth true; echo $?` | **non-zero**, no shell: the key is tunnel-only |
| `ask-cloud` (optional) | aider on Gemini with the laptop's own key |

If `ai-ping` says `BAD TOKEN`, the person re-runs the `read -rs` step with
the right token; `NO ANSWER` means the host is asleep or LM Studio is down
(fix on the host); `DOWN` means no tunnel. If `ssh hearth true` **succeeds**,
stop: the key was authorized without restrictions — tell the person, and do
not continue.

**5. Editors.** Zed: the block in [Zed on the laptop](#zed-on-the-laptop),
with the laptop's token entered through Zed's UI by the person.

**Cursor cannot use hearth as its model provider.** Cursor's backend makes
the model call, not the laptop, so a `localhost` base URL never reaches the
tunnel; the only workaround is a public endpoint, which the *Never* list
forbids, and prompts would still pass through Cursor's servers (checked
2026-10-31). Use Zed, aider or `ask` for local-big on the laptop; Cursor
with its own models only, and not on sensitive repos.

## Zed on the laptop

Same provider as the host, through the tunnel:

```json
{
  "language_models": {
    "openai_compatible": {
      "hearth": {
        "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "available_models": [
          { "name": "local-big", "display_name": "local-big", "max_tokens": 131072 }
        ]
      }
    }
  },
  "agent": { "default_model": { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-big" } }
}
```

Token through the UI (Agent panel → settings → `hearth` → API key): the
**laptop's** token, not the host's. Start `ai-tunnel` before Zed, or Zed's
models show as unavailable.

A Gemini provider **is allowed on the laptop** (D3), with the laptop's own
key. The host has none. Keep sensitive repos on `hearth/local-big`.

Copy the Write/Research profile block from `docs/IDES.md` too if the laptop
uses auslaw: the same "Write enables every server" trap applies.

## Open WebUI from the laptop

`ai-webui` opens `http://127.0.0.1:3000` through the tunnel — the host's own
instance, same login and history. Nothing to install on the laptop.

## Revoking a laptop

1. LM Studio app → Manage Tokens → delete `laptop-<name>`.
2. Host: delete the `hearth-tunnel-<name>` line from `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`.

Either one alone cuts it off; do both.

## Never

- Port-forward `:22`, `:1234` or `:3000` on the router.
- Turn on Serve on Local Network for a laptop — the tunnel makes it unnecessary,
  and it would put the token on the LAN in plain HTTP.
- Copy the host's token or Google key to a laptop.

## Host SSH hardening (recommended, not done for you)

macOS Remote Login accepts passwords by default. With keys in place:

```bash
echo 'PasswordAuthentication no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no' | sudo tee /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/100-hearth.conf
sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/com.openssh.sshd
```

Test a key login from the laptop **before** closing your current session.
# MCP registry

Server → tools → which clients may reach them. This file and the client config
docs (`docs/IDES.md`) are the only places a concrete MCP server may be named.
Update both together, plus an ADR. Since ADR 0016 servers live in the client
(Zed `context_servers`), not the gateway (ADR 0008, superseded).

MCP tools are a **second egress path**. Keeping cloud models off `:1234`
stops a prompt reaching a cloud *model*; it says nothing about a tool that
calls the internet on its own account. That is why every server below declares
where it goes, and why a networked server is enabled only in a profile named
for that egress.

## Registered

| Server | Transport | Egress | Tools | Clients |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `auslaw` | stdio | `austlii.edu.au` | 7 of 10 | host Zed, `research` profile only (ADR 0016). Gateway `research` key until phase 5 |

## auslaw

Australian and NZ case law and legislation. Source: `~/dev/auslaw-mcp`.

**stdio, not HTTP.** Its HTTP mode calls `listen(port)` with no host, so it
binds every interface with no authentication, and defaults to `:3000` — which
is Open WebUI. stdio spawns a child process with no listener at all.

### Tools, by where they go

| Tool | Reaches | Exposed |
|---|---|---|
| `format_citation` | nothing — pure AGLC4 string formatting | yes |
| `search_legislation` | AustLII | yes |
| `search_by_citation` | AustLII | yes |
| `fetch_document_text` | AustLII | yes |
| `generate_pinpoint` | AustLII | yes |
| `validate_citation` | AustLII | yes |
| `search_cases` | AustLII (+ jade.io, disabled) | yes |
| `resolve_jade_article` | jade.io | **no** |
| `jade_citation_lookup` | jade.io | **no** |
| `search_citing_cases` | jade.io | **no** |

The jade.io tools are excluded at the **server** level via `allowed_tools`, so
they are not exposed to any key regardless of its grant, and
`JADE_SESSION_COOKIE` is deliberately absent from the server's environment.
Permission says no, and the credential to do it anyway is not present.

Why exclude them: jade.io access is a reverse-engineered GWT-RPC endpoint
driven by a session cookie lifted from a browser. That is a credential in a
config file and a fragile contract with a commercial provider. AustLII is free,
public, and has neither problem. `search_cases` stays because its jade half
returns an empty array without the cookie while its AustLII half still works.

### Who may reach it

**After ADR 0016 (host Zed, 2026-10-31):** a `context_servers` stdio entry in
`~/.config/zed/settings.json`, tools enabled only in the `research` agent
profile. The built-in Write profile's `enable_all_context_servers` is
overridden to `false`, and each tool call asks first. There is no cloud
provider in host Zed (D3), so tool output only ever re-enters a local model.
See `docs/IDES.md`.

**Gateway era (until phase 5):** `research` only — local models plus these
tools, and no cloud role.

`private` deliberately gets nothing. Its promise is that nothing leaves this
machine, and every useful auslaw tool sends a query to AustLII. Switching to
`research` is the act of saying "this query is going to AustLII" rather than
that being an ambient property of the everyday key.

## Rules these enforce

**Off by default.** Zed's built-in Write profile enables every context server
(`enable_all_context_servers: true` in Zed 1.26); host settings redefine
Write with it `false`. A server's tools are switched on only in a profile
named for its egress (`research` for auslaw).

**No cloud model alongside a networked tool.** Tool output re-enters the
prompt. Host Zed has no cloud provider (D3), so tool output only ever reaches
a local model.

**Ask before each call.** `agent.tool_permissions.default` is `confirm`.

## Checks

There is no automated MCP scope audit since the gateway went (ADR 0016).
By hand, after any Zed upgrade or settings change:

| Check | Proves |
|---|---|
| Agent panel → Write profile shows no auslaw tools | the default profile cannot reach AustLII |
| Research profile shows exactly the 7 allowed tools | the jade.io tools stay off |
| `grep -c JADE ~/.config/zed/settings.json` is 0 | the cookie is not configured |

## Adding a server

1. Read its source and establish what it talks to. That is the whole job; the
   rest is typing.
2. Add it to Zed `context_servers` (stdio preferred — no listener).
3. Enable its tools only in a profile named for its egress; never in Write.
4. Add a row to this file, a note in `docs/IDES.md`, and an ADR.

### Traps

**A wrong path is silent.** A stdio server whose command or script path is
wrong exits immediately and shows no tools — identical in the UI to a
correctly scoped profile. After adding one, confirm its tools appear in the
profile that should have them.

**Zed profile overrides replace, they do not merge.** Overriding a built-in
profile needs `name` and the full tool list, or the whole settings file fails
to load (`missing field name`).
# Migration: LiteLLM + Ollama → LM Studio direct

**Status:** in progress — ADR 0016 accepted 2026-10-31.
**ADR:** `docs/decisions/0016-lmstudio-direct.md`
**Drafted:** 2026-10-31

## How to use this file (for Cursor, Claude Code, Zed, aider)

- Follow `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md`. Plan → operator refines → operator approves
  → build. **One phase at a time.** Do not start a phase until the operator says
  so in the conversation; do not start the next phase until the current gate
  passes and the operator confirms.
- **Stop at every `DECISION` and ask.** Do not pick for the operator.
- Every phase ends with a gate. Record gate output in
  `bench/results/<date>-<phase>/report.md` and tick the phase in the status
  table below in the same commit.
- LM Studio CLI flags below are written from documentation, not tested on this
  Mac. **Confirm each flag with `lms <cmd> --help` before using it**, and
  correct this file if it is wrong.
- Shell rules still apply: bash 3.2, LF only, no `declare -A` / `mapfile` /
  `${x^^}`. Preflight scans for these.
- No secrets in git. Tokens go in `~/.config/hearth/env` (host) and
  `~/.hearth-key` (clients), never in the repo.

## Status

| Phase | What | State |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | Baseline concurrency bench on the current stack | done 2026-10-31 — `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase0/report.md` |
| 1 | LM Studio beside the current stack, localhost only | done 2026-10-31: accepted by operator; wrong-tool picks (3/40) tracked as a metric, not a blocker; no GGUF bench — `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase1/report.md` |
| 2 | GPU wired-limit headroom | skipped 2026-10-31: peak 29.7 GB, memory is not the bottleneck (operator) |
| 3 | Cut host clients over (Zed, aider, WebUI, sandbox, MCP) | done 2026-10-31 — `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase3/report.md` |
| 4 | LAN access with tokens; laptop clients | host side done 2026-10-31 (SSH tunnel, D8); tunnel tested end to end on the host; **gate pending on the laptop** |
| 5 | Stop the old stack; rewrite scripts and docs | done 2026-10-31 — reboot gate passed, `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase5/report.md` |
| 6 | Soak one week, then delete old weights and services | uninstall done 2026-10-31 **without a soak** (operator); backups in `~/hearth-backups/`; ADR 0016 not yet marked accepted-complete |

## Decisions the operator must make

| ID | Question | Recommendation | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|
| D1 | Roles after migration: keep `local-code` and `local-chat` as separate models, or collapse to `local-big` + `local-embed`? | **Collapse.** Two heavy models cannot both stay resident with 3 × 64K slots inside the GPU limit, and evictions are failure F2. Keep the names as client-side aliases that resolve to `local-big` | **Collapse** (2026-10-31) |
| D2 | Slots and context for `local-big` | 3 slots × 64K. Lower to 2 if the phase 1 footprint exceeds the limit | **3 × 64K** (2026-10-31); context raised to 128K by ADR 0017. Idle footprint after that reload was 20.7 GB, so slots stay 3 |
| D3 | Does Zed **on the host** get a Gemini provider? | No on the host (keeps sensitive work local by default); yes on the laptop | **No on host** (2026-10-31) |
| D4 | Digest drift detection | Keep: hash the model directory in `config/roles.tsv` and check it in preflight | **Hash the weights** (2026-10-31): `weights` column in roles.tsv, fast check in preflight, `--deep` re-hash |
| D5 | Keep Open WebUI? | Yes, pointed at `:1234`. It no longer needs the gateway's Postgres | **Keep** (2026-10-31) |
| D6 | Headless runner: LM Studio app with "run server on login", or `llmster`? | `llmster` if it installs cleanly; the app otherwise | **The app** (changed 2026-10-31). Was `llmster`; it worked and honoured the app-set auth, but the app and llmster cannot share `~/.lmstudio` and the operator wants the GUI. The app runs the server with "Enable Local LLM Service (headless)" on; ~1.1 GB extra for its GUI processes. Removing Ollama.app: done (operator) |
| D8 | Phase 4: LAN serving or SSH tunnel? | Tunnel: nothing exposed, encrypted, same on/off LAN | **SSH tunnel only** (2026-10-31). Per-laptop token + `restrict`ed tunnel-only key |
| D7 | Soak length before phase 6 cleanup | — | **No soak** — operator deleted the old stack 2026-10-31 |

## Target

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph host["Mac Studio"]
        zed["Zed agent"]
        aider["aider / ask-*"]
        webui["Open WebUI"]
        sbx["sandboxed.sh"]
        lms["LM Studio :1234<br/>local-big, local-embed"]
    end
    laptop["LAN laptop"]
    cloud["Gemini"]
    zed --> lms
    aider --> lms
    webui --> lms
    sbx --> lms
    laptop -->|"token"| lms
    aider -.->|"ask-cloud"| cloud
```

---

## Phase 0 — Baseline

Purpose: numbers to beat, measured the same way before and after.

1. Write `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` (bash wrapper) + `bench/concurrency.py`
   (stdlib only, `python3 -I`).
   - Args: `--base-url`, `--token-env NAME`, `--model ROLE`, `--concurrency N`
     (default 3), `--rounds R` (default 10), `--prompt-tokens T` (default
     30000).
   - Each request: a realistic agent turn — system prompt + 6 tool schemas
     (`read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `list_directory`, `grep`,
     `run_command`) + filler file content to reach T tokens, `stream: true`,
     asking for a `read_file` tool call.
   - Record per request: time to first byte, time to first token, total time,
     HTTP status, whether a valid tool call came back, finish reason.
   - Client-side timeout 180 s. A timeout is a recorded failure, not a crash.
   - Sample the inference process footprint every 2 s
     (`footprint -p <pid>` or `vmmap --summary`) and record the peak.
   - Output `raw.tsv` + `report.md` (p50/p95 TTFT, failures, peak footprint).
2. Run against the current stack: `--base-url http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1`,
   `--model local-big`, concurrency 1 then 3.

**Gate:** report committed. Failures at concurrency 3 are expected; they are
the baseline.

## Phase 1 — LM Studio beside the current stack

Localhost only. Nothing else changes yet.

**Checked on this Mac (llmster 0.0.27-1, 2026-10-31):**
- Installer: `curl -fsSL https://lmstudio.ai/install.sh` → `~/.lmstudio/bin/lms`.
  Run with `--no-modify-path`; scripts call the full path.
- `lms load` flags: `--identifier`, `-c/--context-length`, `--gpu max`,
  `--parallel <n>` (concurrent predictions is a **load** flag), `--ttl`,
  `--estimate-only`, `-y`.
- `lms server start` flags: `-p/--port`, `--bind` (default `127.0.0.1`;
  `0.0.0.0` for LAN), `--cors`. **No auth flag**; auth and tokens are
  GUI-only (see D6).
- Runtime: `lms runtime get mlx-llm -y`.
- Weights: `lms get <hugging face URL> -y` picks an exact build; a bare
  staff-pick name with `-y` may pick a larger quantisation.

1. Install LM Studio 0.4.x (or `llmster`, per D6). Record the version in
   `docs/MODELS.md`.
2. Download the MLX 4-bit build of the current `local-big` weights and the
   embedding model. Exact model keys go in `config/roles.tsv` and
   `docs/MODELS.md` only.
3. Create `config/roles.tsv` (tab-separated, `#` comments allowed):
   ```
   # role        backend    model_key                 ctx    slots
   local-big     lmstudio   <mlx model key>           131072 3
   local-embed   lmstudio   <embedding model key>     2048   1
   cloud-cheap   gemini     <gemini model id>         -      -
   ```
4. Write `scripts/lms-load.sh`: reads `config/roles.tsv`, unloads anything not
   listed, loads each `lmstudio` row with `--identifier <role>` and
   `--context-length <ctx>` and GPU offload max. Set concurrent predictions to
   `slots` (confirm whether that is a load flag or a server setting).
   Idempotent.
5. Server settings: port 1234, **serve on local network OFF** for now, require
   authentication **ON**, CORS **OFF**, JIT model loading **OFF**, auto-unload
   **OFF**, "allow calling servers from mcp.json" **OFF**.
6. Create a host token; store it as `LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN` in
   `~/.config/hearth/env` (mode 600).
7. Run `scripts/tool-smoke.sh` and `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` against
   `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` at concurrency 1 and 3.
8. Stop Ollama's runner while benching (`ollama stop` or unload) so both
   servers are not competing for GPU memory; note it in the report.

**Gate (all must hold):**
- tool-smoke passes.
- Concurrency 3 × 10 rounds: zero timeouts, zero invalid tool calls.
- p95 TTFT at concurrency 3 is better than the phase 0 baseline.
- Peak footprint stays at least 4 GB under the current wired limit. If not,
  drop to 2 slots (D2) and rerun.
- A request with no token gets 401.

## Phase 2 — GPU headroom (optional, measure)

1. Read the current limit: `sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb` (0 means the default,
   ~75% of RAM).
2. Try `sudo sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb=56000` and rerun the phase 1
   concurrency bench.
3. If it helps, add a root launchd daemon `com.hearth.wired-limit` that sets
   it at boot (`scripts/install-wired-limit.sh --apply/--check/--remove`). It
   resets on reboot otherwise.

**Gate:** bench numbers recorded with and without. Keep only if better and
the system stays responsive with Zed, a browser and the bench running.

## Phase 3 — Host clients

1. **Zed (`settings.json`)** — replace the `hearth` provider:
   ```json
   "language_models": {
     "openai_compatible": {
       "hearth": {
         "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
         "available_models": [
           { "name": "local-big", "display_name": "local-big", "max_tokens": 131072 }
         ]
       }
     }
   }
   ```
   Token goes in through the Zed UI (keychain). Set the agent default, thread
   summary model, commit message model and inline assistant model to
   `hearth/local-big` so nothing asks for another model. Remove `cloud-cheap`
   from the host per D3. Update `docs/IDES.md`.
2. **MCP** — move `auslaw` from `litellm/config.yaml` into Zed
   `context_servers` as a stdio server with the same allowed tools and without
   the jade cookie. Update `docs/MCP.md` (registry stays; "keys" column becomes
   "clients").
3. **aider / aliases** — rewrite `scripts/aliases.zsh`:
   - `HEARTH_LOCAL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1`, token from
     `LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN`, injected per invocation (keep the no-export rule).
   - `ask`, `ask-local`, `ask-big`, `ask-chat`, `ask-fast` all →
     `openai/local-big` (D1). `ask-arch` becomes `--architect` with both models
     `local-big` (same model, no eviction).
   - `ask-cloud` reads the Gemini model id from `config/roles.tsv` and uses
     `GOOGLE_API_KEY` directly. It is the only alias that can leave the host.
   - `ai-loaded` → `lms ps`. `ai-restart` → restart LM Studio's server.
4. **Open WebUI** (`scripts/webui.sh`) — point at
   `http://host.containers.internal:1234/v1` with the host token. Confirm
   containers can reach the host port with "serve on local network" still off;
   if not, note it and revisit in phase 4.
5. **Sandbox** — `scripts/sandboxed.sh` and `scripts/sandbox-probe.sh`: egress
   allow-list changes from `127.0.0.1:4000` to `127.0.0.1:1234`. The probe must
   still prove that nothing else is reachable.

**Gate:** a real Zed agent session (≥ 20 tool calls, two threads at once)
completes with no stall; aider `ask` and `ask-cloud` work; sandbox-probe
passes; WebUI answers.

## Phase 4 — LAN

**Decided 2026-10-31 (D8): SSH tunnel only.** The steps below describe LAN
serving and are kept for the record; what was built instead:

- Host stays bound to `127.0.0.1` (Serve on Local Network off).
- `scripts/client-setup.sh <name>` prints laptop steps; `--authorize` adds the
  laptop's key to `authorized_keys` as
  `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen="127.0.0.1:1234",permitopen="127.0.0.1:3000",permitlisten="none:1"`.
  `permitlisten` was added the same day after review: `permitopen` limits `-L` only, and `port-forwarding` had re-enabled `-R`.
  OpenSSH 10.5 rejects a port of 0 in that option (`invalid permission port`), so the dummy listen is `none:1`, a host the client never sends.
  The earlier throwaway-sshd test still holds for the forced command (shell refused, `-L` to :8888 refused, :1234 and :3000 work). `permitlisten="none:1"` was retested the same way: `-R` is refused and `-L` to :1234 still works.
- `client/hearth-client.sh` / `.ps1`: `ai-tunnel`, `ai-ping`, `ai-models`,
  `ai-ask`, `ai-webui`, `ask*` → `local-big`, `ask-cloud` with the laptop's
  own key. Secrets read from mode-600 files per command, never exported.
  `client/hearth-webui.sh` removed — the laptop uses the host's WebUI.
- `docs/LAPTOP.md` rewritten.

**Gate (adjusted for the tunnel):** from the laptop — `ai-ping` up, a wrong
token refused, `ai-models` lists the roles, a Zed agent session works,
`ask-cloud` works with the laptop's key, the tunnel key cannot open a shell.
From the host — `healthcheck.sh` section 5 still reports no LAN answer.


1. LM Studio: serve on local network **ON**. Authentication stays on.
2. One token per machine: `laptop-<name>`. Store on the laptop in
   `~/.hearth-key` (mode 600).
3. macOS firewall: allow LM Studio incoming. Never port-forward 1234.
4. Rewrite `client/hearth-client.sh`, `client/hearth-client.ps1`,
   `client/hearth-webui.sh`, `scripts/client-setup.sh`:
   - `HEARTH_GATEWAY` → `HEARTH_HOST` (e.g. `http://192.168.1.10:1234`); keep
     reading the old variable name for one release.
   - `ai-ping` checks `/v1/models` with the token (200 = up, 401 = bad token,
     no answer = host asleep).
   - `ai-models` lists identifiers from `/v1/models`.
   - `ask-cloud` on the laptop uses the laptop's own `GOOGLE_API_KEY`.
5. Off-LAN: document `ssh -N -L 1234:127.0.0.1:1234 USER@HOST`.
6. Rewrite `docs/LAPTOP.md` (Zed block, aider block, tunnel).

**Gate:** from the laptop — `ai-ping` up, request without token refused,
Zed agent session works, `ask-cloud` works with the laptop's key. From the
host — `nc -z <lan-ip> 1234` answers and an unauthenticated request is 401.

## Phase 5 — Retire the old stack, rewrite tooling

**Done early (2026-10-31, operator):** Ollama stopped (`launchctl bootout`) and
disabled (`launchctl disable gui/<uid>/com.colinnevin.ollama`); `Ollama.app` in
the Trash. Plist and model weights untouched. Rollback: Put Back the app,
`launchctl enable …`, then `launchctl bootstrap gui/<uid>
~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.colinnevin.ollama.plist`.

1. Stop and disable (do not uninstall yet): LiteLLM launchd agent, Ollama
   launchd service, `com.hearth.mlx-watchdog`, the Postgres container. Back up
   the Postgres volume to `~/hearth-backups/` first.
2. Delete scripts that only exist for the gateway or Ollama:
   `create-keys.sh`, `db.sh`, `setup-prisma.sh`, `deploy.sh`,
   `audit-fallbacks.sh`, `audit-mcp-scope.sh`, `bisect-config.sh`,
   `install-ollama-service.sh`, `lock-ollama.sh`, `mlx-watchdog.sh`,
   `apply-nothink.sh`, `test-nothink.sh`, `usage.sh`.
3. Rewrite against LM Studio and `config/roles.tsv`:
   `lib.sh`, `boot.sh`, `install-autostart.sh`, `status.sh`, `diagnose.sh`,
   `healthcheck.sh`, `preflight.sh`, `bench.sh`, `tool-smoke.sh`,
   `pin-digests.sh` (per D4), `swap-role.sh`, `pull-candidates.sh`
   (`lms get`), `mlx-bench.sh`.
   - `healthcheck.sh` must check: server up; each role answers; request
     without token → 401; LAN interface answers only with token; nothing but
     LM Studio listens on 1234; JIT loading is off (a request for an unlisted
     model fails rather than loading it).
     *Measured 2026-10-31: it does not fail — LM Studio answers an unknown
     model name with the loaded LLM. Nothing loads; healthcheck warns.*
   - `preflight.sh` keeps CRLF / bash-3.2 scans; replaces config checks with
     `config/roles.tsv` ↔ `docs/MODELS.md` consistency and the D4 drift check.
4. Keep unchanged: `searxng.sh`, `install-global-rules.sh`.
5. Remove `litellm/` from the tree (history keeps it).
6. Docs: rewrite `README.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/ROUTING.md`,
   `docs/RUNBOOK.md`, `docs/MODELS.md`, `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` (privacy is now
   "local server holds only local models" + sandbox egress, not key scope).
   Mark ADRs 0002, 0003, 0008, 0011, 0013, 0015 superseded by 0016.
7. `CLAUDE.md` hard rules:
   - Rule 1: concrete names in `config/roles.tsv` and `docs/MODELS.md`;
     MCP servers in Zed config docs and `docs/MCP.md`.
   - Rule 2: no router means no fallbacks. Replace with "LM Studio never holds
     a cloud model, and no script adds a remote backend to `config/roles.tsv`
     except under `backend: gemini` (or another explicit cloud backend)".
   - Rule 3: restate as above.
   - Rule 6: per D4.
   - Rule 7: update the resident set figure from the phase 1 measurement.

**Gate:** `preflight.sh` and `healthcheck.sh` pass on a clean reboot with
only LM Studio running. `grep -rn '4000\|11434\|litellm\|ollama'` over
`scripts/ client/ docs/*.md` returns only intentional historical mentions.

## Phase 6 — Soak and clean up

After the soak agreed in D7 with no stall:

1. Uninstall LiteLLM (pipx), Ollama, the Podman Postgres container and volume
   (backup kept), and delete Ollama model weights.
2. Remove the stopped launchd plists.
3. Mark ADR 0016 accepted; update `docs/PLAN.md`.

If a stall recurs during the soak: capture `lms log stream` output, footprint,
and the concurrency bench at the failing settings before changing anything.
# MLX stage-1 bench (Ollama vs Rapid-MLX)

> **Historical.** Rapid-MLX vs Ollama (ADR 0012/0013). Local inference is now LM Studio — ADR 0016, `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase1`.

Compare **agent tool loops** — not raw tok/s. Zed needs read → tool result →
write with messy file content. Code bench (`scripts/bench.sh`) does not catch
the Qwen XML failure mode.

**Date drafted:** 2026-09-06. **2026-10-22:** `local-big` moved back to Ollama's
MLX runner after the same tool-smoke passed there (ADR 0013). This file is for
a side experiment. It is not how the role is served.

## Envelope (this Mac)

| Constraint | Value |
|---|---|
| Unified RAM | 64 GB |
| GPU-accessible | ~48 GB |
| Hot models | 1 Ollama model (ADR 0011). A Rapid-MLX process beside it spends the GPU budget |

| MLX alias (Rapid-MLX) | Weights (approx) | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `qwen3.6-35b-4bit` | ~20 GB | bench only — role is the Ollama tag in `docs/MODELS.md` |
| `gpt-oss-20b` | ~13 GB | bench / break-glass |
| `gemma-4-12b-4bit` | ~8 GB | candidate — not promoted |

## Install Rapid-MLX

```bash
scripts/mlx-bench.sh --install-rapid
scripts/mlx-bench.sh --serve qwen3.6-35b-4bit   # foreground, :8000, not launchd
```

Stop it when the experiment is done so it does not sit beside Ollama.

**Gemma 4** MLX weights need the vision stack:

```bash
pipx inject rapid-mlx 'mlx-vlm==0.6.17'
```

## Tool smoke (automated)

Latest results: `bench/results/2026-09-06-1229-tool-smoke/`

```bash
scripts/tool-smoke.sh openai http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1 qwen3.6-35b-4bit
scripts/tool-smoke.sh ollama gpt-oss:20b
scripts/tool-smoke.sh ollama qwen3-coder:30b
```

Pass = **turn 2 emits `write_file` or `edit_file`** with `<` in content.

## Zed

Use the **`hearth`** provider — agent default `local-big`, which is Ollama
(ADR 0013).

For raw MLX A/B (bypass gateway):

```json
"mlx": {
  "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1",
  "available_models": [
    { "name": "qwen3.6-35b-4bit", "display_name": "qwen3.6-35b-4bit (MLX direct)", "max_tokens": 65536 }
  ]
}
```

## Decision rules

| Outcome | Action |
|---|---|
| Candidate fails tool smoke | Do not promote |
| Passes + Zed manual OK | ADR + `docs/MODELS.md` + config. Qwen3.6 is already `local-big` via Ollama (ADR 0013) |

Nothing else becomes a role until `scripts/swap-role.sh` + ADR.
# Model registry

Role → model. This file and `config/roles.tsv` are the only places a concrete
model name is written by hand. Update both together, plus an ADR. Generated
copies: Zed `display_name` labels (`scripts/zed-sync.sh`) and the key plus
weights path in `pins/digests.tsv` (`scripts/pin-digests.sh`). (Before ADR 0016
the second file was `litellm/config.yaml`.)

**Hardware envelope:** 64 GB unified. macOS caps GPU-accessible memory at ~75% above 36 GB,
so the real ceiling is **~48 GB**, not the full 64. The resident set matters more than any single
model's size — see the measurement below.

## Live — LM Studio (ADR 0016)

The LM Studio app 0.4.28 owns the server (D6). Machine-read copy:
`config/roles.tsv`.

| Role | Model key | Source | Size | Load | Set | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `local-big` | `qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b` | `lmstudio-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MLX-4bit` @ `0c4a20a` | 20.4 GB | 128K × 3 parallel, draft MTP | 2026-10-31 | Same base model as the Ollama-era agent; MLX 4-bit from lmstudio-community (quantisation may differ from the Ollama build). The only local chat/agent role (D1). Context raised from 64K the same day (ADR 0017): the model card asks for at least 128K so thinking holds. Native window is 262,144 |
| `cloud-cheap` | `gemini-3.6-flash` | Google, `ask-cloud` only | — | — | 2026-10-22 | 1M ctx, 65K output. ADR 0014 |

**Resident set:** 20.7 GB idle at 128K × 3 with `local-embed` still loaded,
measured 2026-10-31 (ADR 0017). That role was removed 2026-11-01 (ADR 0018);
its weights were 0.3 GB. The 64K concurrency-3 peak was 29.7 GB
(`bench/results/2026-10-31-phase1`); that bench was not re-run at 128K.
The slot count stays 3.

`local-code` and `local-chat` were collapsed into `local-big` on 2026-10-31
(D1). The Ollama weights were deleted the same day with the rest of the old
stack. Known quality gap
against the Ollama build: 3 of 40 concurrency-bench turns picked `grep`
instead of the requested `read_file` (0 of 40 on Ollama); tracked, not
blocking.

**Weight drift:** recorded and checked — see Weight pinning below (D4).

**Bench candidate (not a role):** `qwen3.6:27b-coding-mtp-q4_K_M` on Ollama
(~18 GB) — rejected for verbosity vs `qwen3-coder:30b` (ADR 0005 era bench).

## Dormant

No keys. In the gateway era these were commented-out blocks in `config.yaml`;
after ADR 0016 a cloud role would be a `gemini` (or other explicit cloud
backend) row in `config/roles.tsv` plus an alias that passes its own key —
never a model loaded into LM Studio.

| Role | Model | Blocked on |
|---|---|---|
| `cloud-code` | `claude-sonnet-5` | Anthropic API key — **deliberately unset**. A Console key bills separately from any Claude subscription. Only worth opening if aider, Zed or a laptop should reach Claude through the gateway |
| `cloud-frontier` | `kimi-k3` via OpenRouter | OpenRouter key. Primary frontier route |
| `cloud-frontier-direct` | `kimi-k3` via Moonshot | Moonshot key. Same price, best K3 caching |
| `cloud-frontier-fast` | `kimi-k3` via Fireworks | Fireworks key. Dearest; throughput or LoRA only |


There is **no local Kimi K3**. The smallest community quant is ~540 GB at 1.56
bits, and Ollama cannot load it — support is an open llama.cpp pull request.
Frontier is cloud-only by necessity, which is what these roles are for.

## Retired

| Model | Was | Removed | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen3.6:35b-mlx` (Ollama) | `local-big` | 2026-10-31 | Same base model now served by LM Studio (lmstudio-community MLX 4-bit). ADR 0016 |
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | `local-code` | 2026-10-31 | Role collapsed into `local-big` (D1). ADR 0016 |
| `qwen3:8b` | `local-chat` | 2026-10-31 | Role collapsed into `local-big` (D1). ADR 0016 |
| `nomic-embed-text` (Ollama, then LM Studio f16 GGUF) | `local-embed` | 2026-11-01 | Nothing called it. Healthcheck was the only request. ADR 0018 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | `local-big` | 2026-09-06 | Slower tool-smoke than the Qwen3.6 agent. ADR 0012, then 0013 |
| `llama3.3:70b` | `local-big` | 2026-08-02 | 42 GB, could not stay resident. 440 s to first reply. ADR 0004 |
| `qwen2.5-coder:32b` | `local-code` | 2026-08-02 | Superseded by the 30B MoE at the same size |
| `qwen2.5-coder:7b` | `local-fast` | 2026-08-02 | Role retired — the MoE made a separate fast coder redundant. ADR 0005 |

Note on the last one: Qwen3-Coder has **no variant below 30B**, so
`qwen2.5-coder:7b` remained the best sub-10 GB coding model available. It went
because the role became unnecessary, not because it was outdated. If a small
dense coder appears, the role can return.

## Verbosity, not speed

Second measurement, from the first real bench run:

| Model | tokens over 4 tasks | wall clock | avg tok/s |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | 1,218 | 11.2 s | 108.8 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | 4,466 (3.7x) | 47.6 s | 93.8 |
| `qwen3:8b` (thinking on) | 13,840 (11.4x) | 181.4 s | 76.3 |

Throughput barely moves — 65–120 tok/s across everything, because the machine
is memory-bandwidth bound. **Wall clock is set by how many tokens a model emits,
not how fast.** A hybrid reasoning model can be the slowest role while being the
smallest.

Disabling thinking on `local-chat` took it from 2,370 tokens / 29.7 s to
**386 tokens / 5.1 s** through the gateway. See ADR 0006.

## The measurement that drove all of this

Through the gateway, one short prompt each, from cold:

| Role | Before | Time | After | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `local-code` | qwen2.5-coder:32b | 29 s | qwen3-coder:30b | **6 s** |
| `local-big` | llama3.3:70b | **440 s** | gpt-oss:20b | **5 s** |
| `cloud-cheap` | gemini-3.5-flash | 2 s | gemini-3.5-flash | 1 s |

**440 s to 5 s — not a faster model, a resident one.**

The old set needed 65.7 GB against a ~48 GB GPU ceiling, so something was always
evicted and reaching for `local-big` meant reloading 42 GB from disk. That
Ollama-era set totalled 37.5 GB. The live resident set is the figure at the
top of this file.

The lesson worth keeping: on a fixed-memory machine the **resident set** matters
more than any individual model's quality. A 70B that evicts your coding model is
slower in practice than a 20B that never leaves RAM, whatever the benchmarks say.
MoE architectures are what make the newer set fit — ~3B active parameters gives
near-30B reasoning at 7B speed.

Disk: 63 GB before, ~37 GB after.

## Cost reference

Verified 2026-08-01. LiteLLM auto-syncs its own cost map; this table is for human
decisions and does **not** update itself. Re-check before relying on it.

| Kimi K3 via | $/M in | $/M out | Note |
|---|---|---|---|
| Moonshot (direct) | 3.00 | 15.00 | source of truth |
| OpenRouter | 3.00 | 15.00 | ~92% cache hit reported → effective input ≈ 0.52 |
| Fireworks serverless | 3.30 | 16.50 | 10% dearer; buys throughput and LoRA |

- **Fireworks is the dearest of the three.** This stack originally defaulted to it
  because it was the provider that came to mind first, not because it won on
  price. That is exactly the failure mode this registry exists to catch.
- **Output price is the one that bites** — it compounds through agentic loops.

## Weight pinning (D4)

`scripts/pin-digests.sh` hashes each role's `weights` path (column 6 of
`config/roles.tsv`, under `~/.lmstudio/models`) into `pins/digests.tsv`:
file count, bytes, newest mtime, and a sha256 over every file. Git-tracked,
so drift shows in a diff.

```bash
scripts/pin-digests.sh                  # record, after a bench you trust (~40 s)
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check          # fast; preflight runs this
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check --deep   # re-hash everything
```

Recorded 2026-10-31. This detects, it does not enforce — LM Studio loads
whatever is on disk. The embedding pin was removed with the role (ADR 0018).

## Changing a role

1. Download the candidate with an exact source: `lms get <hugging face URL> -y`
2. Bench it against the incumbent: `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` and
   `bench/tasks/` (rule 5). Same settings as the last baseline
3. Check the footprint fits under the ~48 GB ceiling with `local-big` already resident
4. Update this table and `config/roles.tsv` together, then `scripts/lms-load.sh`
   (which also refreshes Zed's labels via `scripts/zed-sync.sh`)
5. `scripts/tool-smoke.sh openai http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1 local-big`
6. Write an ADR in `docs/decisions/` and a row in `docs/HISTORY.md`
7. Delete the old weights only once the replacement has proven itself

`scripts/swap-role.sh <role> <model key> <weights path> [ctx] [slots]` does
steps 4–5 and reverts on failure; `scripts/bench.sh <model key>` loads a
candidate as `bench-candidate` (`--evict` if it will not fit beside
`local-big`); `scripts/pull-candidates.sh <url> …` sizes downloads against
disk and the GPU ceiling.
# Build status

> **Historical (gateway era).** Superseded by ADR 0016. Current status: `docs/MIGRATION-LMSTUDIO.md`; what was tried: `docs/HISTORY.md`.

Last updated: 2026-08-02

**Not the source of truth.** Current design: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, then
`docs/MIGRATION-LMSTUDIO.md` and `docs/MODELS.md`. This file is the
gateway-era plan. The original plan is preserved in
`docs/decisions/0001-inherited-state.md`.

## Where things stand

| Phase | State |
|---|---|
| 0 — capture inherited state | done — ADR 0001 |
| 1 — repo scaffold | done |
| 2 — role-based gateway config | **deployed and serving** |
| Postgres via Podman on :5433 | done, running |
| Virtual keys | done — four created, scoped |
| Leak test | **passing** — boundary enforced, not just documented |
| Aliases switched to roles | done |
| 3 — lock Ollama to localhost | **done** — LAN unreachable, verified |
| llama3.3:70b retired | done — ADR 0004. 440 s to 5 s |
| 4 — bench harness | done and run — found the local-chat verbosity problem |
| local-chat thinking disabled | done — ADR 0006. 29.7 s to 5.1 s |
| digest pinning | recorded + drift-checked (not enforceable in Ollama) |
| 5 — auto-update pipeline | deferred |
| 6 — Open WebUI | **done** — chat UI on :3000, private-scoped, local-chat default |
| Web search via SearXNG | **done** — opt-in per message, :8888, verified end to end |
| `usage.sh` visibility | done — role, tier, model, tokens, LOCAL/CLOUD split |
| 7 — verification tooling | done — preflight, deploy, healthcheck, audit |

## What changed from the original plan

- **Frontier providers: three, not one.** Fireworks was the original default purely
  because it was the provider first mentioned. Verified 2026-08-01, it is the
  dearest: $3.30/$16.50 versus $3.00/$15.00 from both OpenRouter and Moonshot
  direct. OpenRouter is now primary — one key, day-zero access to new models.
- **`cloud-code` is dormant by choice.** No Anthropic API key. A Console key bills
  separately from a Claude subscription, so the gateway route only earns its
  place if aider, Zed or a laptop should reach Claude through the gateway.
- **Four keys, not three.** Added `lan-cloud` so the laptop can reach cloud models
  without widening the everyday `lan` key.
- **Postgres in Podman, not Docker Desktop.**
- **`ask` now defaults to local.** The old `ask` pointed at Anthropic with no key
  set, so it was broken. The safe default on a privacy-first stack should be the
  private one; cloud is now the explicit reach (`ask-cloud`).
- **Deployment does not touch launchd.** `run-litellm.sh` already loads
  `~/.config/litellm/config.yaml`, so that path becomes a symlink into the repo.
  Rollback is one `mv`.

## Bugs the harness caught before deploy

Kept as a record of why the tooling exists.

1. **Fallback leak** in the inherited config — `local-ollama → gemini` meant any
   Ollama timeout silently sent the prompt to Google.
2. **`ollama/` vs `ollama_chat/`** — the completion-API prefix causes tool-call
   loops and dropped context.
3. **Preflight reported a fake leak** when PyYAML was missing. A check that cannot
   run must never look like a finding; ERROR and FAIL are now distinct exit codes.
4. **Env check read the wrong environment** — it consulted the interactive shell,
   but launchd only sources `~/.config/litellm/env`. A false green.
5. **Model presence check was tag-naive** — `nomic-embed-text` did not match
   `nomic-embed-text:latest`.
6. **launchd detection** used `launchctl list | grep`, which misses gui-domain
   agents; it reported "not loaded" while the gateway was serving traffic.
7. **`db.sh` went missing** from disk and nothing noticed. Preflight now verifies
   every referenced script exists.
8. **Leak test could false-pass** — it probed a dormant role, where "model not
   found" also returns 4xx. Now probes a live cloud role and adds a positive
   control, since a merely broken key also refuses everything.
9. **`model_info: tier: local`** — `tier` is a real LiteLLM field accepting only
   `free` or `paid`. Pydantic rejected it and LiteLLM logged "ignoring and
   continuing with other deployments", silently dropping EVERY model. The
   gateway started clean, passed readiness, and served nothing.
10. **bash 4 syntax on bash 3.2** — `declare -A` and unguarded `"${arr[@]}"`
    pass everywhere except macOS, which is the only place it runs.
11. **`curl | python - args <<'PY'`** — the heredoc and the pipe both claim
    stdin, so `json.load(sys.stdin)` reads nothing. Symptom is a bogus
    "response was not JSON".
12. **`/key/delete` accepted a token hash and did nothing** — it wants
    `key_aliases`. A silent no-op that looks like success.
13. **Open WebUI env vars were overridden by its database.** Env only seeds a
    fresh DB; the persisted volume kept the first run's settings. `podman exec
    ... env` showed the feature enabled while the UI had it off.

## Next

1. **Re-run `bench.sh`** now that thinking is off, so the recorded numbers
   match reality, then **read the task 03 output.** `gpt-oss:20b` earned `local-big` on
   capacity, not quality — nothing has yet confirmed it reasons well. Task 03
   hides a float-for-currency bug in money arithmetic; a model that fixes
   the obvious `range(len())` and misses that is the wrong tool for
   production financial code. Syntax checks cannot answer this. It needs a human read.
2. **Record digests after that read**, with `scripts/pin-digests.sh`, so drift
   is detectable from a known-good state rather than an arbitrary one.
3. **Live with it for a week** before building the auto-updater. It is the
   piece most likely to be overbuilt for a problem that turns out to be small.

**Gaps identified 2026-08-02** by comparing against other setups
(`docs/COMPARISON.md`):

- ~~**No sandboxing.**~~ **Closed 2026-08-02** — `scripts/sandboxed.sh` denies
  egress to everything but the gateway and confines writes to the working
  directory. Verified on macOS 26.4, 10/10 checks. ADR 0007.
- ~~**No prompt-injection threat model.**~~ **Written** — `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`.
  Conclusion: detection does not work, so the model exists to say which
  capabilities to cut rather than what to filter.
- **Untrusted MCP servers are unaudited.** Any MCP that fetches from the open
  web and sits next to real client data needs a tool-surface audit before an
  agent is allowed to call it. Highest value per effort of anything left.
- **No confirmation gates** on consequential actions beyond git commits. Costs
  nothing today because nothing in the stack can send mail or move money.
- **No local embeddings.** `local-embed` was removed 2026-11-01 (ADR 0018).
  A reference corpus would be a new role, benched first.

Optional, when the need arises:

- Enable `cloud-code` if aider, Zed or a laptop should reach Claude — needs a
  Console API key, which bills separately from any subscription.
- Enable `cloud-frontier` (OpenRouter) for Kimi K3 and day-zero access to new
  models. There is no local K3 at any quantisation.
- A second Open WebUI without search configured, if you want "no web search on
  sensitive work" to be structural rather than remembered.
# Review handoff: the LM Studio migration

For an AI reviewer (Cursor, Zed, Claude Code) working in a clone of this repo.
Written 2026-10-31. **Review only: change nothing, run nothing that mutates
state.** Report findings; the operator decides what gets fixed.

## Scope

```bash
git log --oneline 8e9ecf2..HEAD     # ~20 commits, all 2026-10-31
git diff --stat 8e9ecf2 HEAD        # bench/results/ is most of the line count — skim it
```

The hearth stack moved from **LiteLLM + Ollama + Postgres** (a gateway on
`:4000`) to **the LM Studio app serving directly on `127.0.0.1:1234`**. The
old stack is deleted, the scripts are rewritten, host clients are cut over,
and laptops reach the host over an SSH tunnel only. Read these first, in this
order:

1. `CLAUDE.md` — the hard rules. Everything is reviewed against them.
2. `docs/decisions/0016-lmstudio-direct.md` — the decision.
3. `docs/MIGRATION-LMSTUDIO.md` — phases, status table, decisions D1–D8.
4. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/ROUTING.md`, `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`.

## What to review, highest risk first

| # | Area | Files | Questions |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Privacy by placement** | `config/roles.tsv`, `scripts/lms-load.sh`, `scripts/aliases.zsh`, `client/*`, `scripts/webui.sh`, `scripts/sandboxed.sh` | Can anything sent to `:1234` reach a cloud model? Does any path other than `ask-cloud` carry a cloud key? Can a sandboxed agent reach anything but `127.0.0.1:1234`? |
| 2 | **Secrets** | `scripts/lib.sh` (`ai_load_env`, `hearth_http`), `client/hearth-client.sh` / `.ps1`, `scripts/aliases.zsh`, `scripts/preflight.sh` | Is a token or key ever exported, put on a command line (visible in `ps`), logged, or written somewhere other than a mode-600 file? `hearth_http` passes it via `curl -K -` for this reason. The `.ps1` sets `$env:` vars for the child — is the cleanup sound? |
| 3 | **Laptop access (D8)** | `scripts/client-setup.sh`, `docs/LAPTOP.md`, the `CLAUDE.md` "Which machine" block | The `authorized_keys` line is `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen=...`. Is the key really tunnel-only? Does `--authorize` refuse and back up correctly? Are the AI-assisted setup instructions safe for a cloud assistant (never sees a secret)? |
| 4 | **Server exposure** | `scripts/boot.sh`, `scripts/healthcheck.sh` | LM Studio rewrites its saved settings on `lms server start`. Is `127.0.0.1` + no CORS enforced on every start path, and checked? Does each negative test (401, no LAN, no CORS, JIT off) have a positive control? |
| 5 | **Login autostart** | `scripts/install-autostart.sh`, `scripts/boot.sh` | launchd `RunAtLoad` + `AbandonProcessGroup` (without it, launchd killed the Podman VM). Failure modes if LM Studio is slow to start, or Podman is missing? |
| 6 | **Exit codes** | every script | `0` pass, `1` real problem, `2` could not run. Can a missing dependency ever report as `1`, or a failure as `0`? |
| 7 | **Portability** | `scripts/*.sh`, `client/hearth-client.sh` | macOS `/bin/bash` 3.2: no `declare -A`, `mapfile`, `${x^^}`; empty arrays guarded under `set -u`. `client/hearth-client.sh` must run in bash *and* zsh on macOS and Linux. Python in heredocs: no heredoc + pipe both on stdin. |
| 8 | **Model-name rule** | everything | Concrete model names only in `config/roles.tsv` and `docs/MODELS.md` (plus generated Zed labels and redacted bench results). `grep` for any that leaked. |
| 9 | **Weight pinning (D4)** | `scripts/pin-digests.sh`, `pins/digests.tsv` | Does `--check` really detect a changed file? Does any doc imply roles are *immutable* rather than drift-detected? |
| 10 | **Docs drift** | `README.md`, `docs/*.md` | Any instruction still pointing at `:4000`, `:11434`, LiteLLM, virtual keys, Postgres, Ollama or `llmster` *as current*? Superseded ADRs and docs marked **Historical** are expected to mention them. Do the docs match what the scripts actually do? |
| 11 | **Benches** | `bench/concurrency.py`, `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh`, `bench/results/2026-10-31-*` | Do the reports' conclusions follow from the numbers? Is the c3 TTFT comparison (LM Studio vs Ollama) like for like? |

## Known and accepted — don't report these as new

- No soak week before deleting the old stack (operator's call). Rollback is
  not possible; ADR 0016 says so.
- With JIT off, an unknown model name is answered by `local-big` instead of
  refused. `healthcheck.sh` warns about it.
- LM Studio auth and tokens are GUI-only; there is no CLI (lmstudio-ai/lms#489).
- Prefill is serialised even with `--parallel 3`; recorded in the phase 0/1 reports.
- Phase 4's laptop gate has not been run yet.
- Cursor cannot use hearth as a model provider (its backend makes the calls).

## Also changed, outside the repo (host only)

Described so you don't have to look. Ask the operator before opening any of
these, and never open the ones in the next section.

- `~/.config/zed/settings.json` — `hearth` provider at `127.0.0.1:1234`,
  every model slot `hearth/local-big`, Write profile fully redefined,
  Research profile with only the auslaw tools.
- `~/.aider.conf.yml` — `openai/local-big` for model and weak model.
- `~/.zshrc` — sources `scripts/aliases.zsh`; Ollama exports removed.
- `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.hearth.boot.plist` — written by `install-autostart.sh`.
- `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` — gets one restricted line per laptop.

## Off limits

You are probably a cloud model; whatever you read goes to your vendor.
**Never open, print, grep or copy:**

- `~/.config/hearth/env` — LM Studio token and Google key
- `~/.zshenv.local*` — a backup there still holds a key
- `~/hearth-backups/` — old database dumps
- `~/.lmstudio/.internal/` — server settings, token hashes
- `~/.hearth-key`, `~/.hearth-gemini-key`, `~/.ssh/` on any machine

## What you may run

Read-only, on the host only, all safe to repeat (exit codes as above):

```bash
scripts/preflight.sh
scripts/healthcheck.sh
scripts/install-autostart.sh --check
scripts/lms-load.sh --check
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check
scripts/zed-sync.sh --check
bash -n scripts/*.sh client/hearth-client.sh
```

**Don't run:** `boot.sh`, `lms-load.sh` (without `--check`),
`install-autostart.sh --apply/--remove`, `client-setup.sh --authorize/--install`,
`swap-role.sh`, `bench.sh`, `concurrency-bench.sh`, `pull-candidates.sh`,
`pin-digests.sh` (without `--check`), `zed-sync.sh` (without `--check`),
anything with `lms load/unload/server`, `podman`, `launchctl` or `sudo`.
On a laptop, run none of `scripts/`.

If you're Zed on `local-big` (128K context, ADR 0017), review one area from the table
per thread rather than the whole diff at once.

## How to report

One finding per item, most severe first:

- **Severity:** critical (privacy or secret leak) / high (wrong result,
  broken check) / medium / low (docs, style)
- **Where:** `path:line`
- **What:** one sentence
- **Failure scenario:** concrete inputs or state → what goes wrong
- **Confidence:** confirmed (you ran or traced it) / plausible

Say which areas you reviewed and which you didn't. "No findings" for an area
is a useful answer, but only with a line on what you checked.
# Routing rules

The decision table. When in doubt, local.

## What must stay local

Route to `local-*` roles (LM Studio, `:1234`), always:

- Any repo or path you have flagged as holding sensitive or private data
- Names, addresses, contact details, financial figures, ID documents
- Anything covered by applicable privacy obligations
- Credentials, keys, `.env` contents, database dumps
- Anything you would not paste into a public forum

## What can go to cloud

- Open-source repos and public code
- Synthetic or anonymised examples
- Language, framework, and API questions with no data attached
- Architecture and design discussion in the abstract
- This repo itself

## Roles

| Role | Use for | Reached by | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| `local-big` | everything local: code, agent, chat, long context | LM Studio `:1234` | live |
| `cloud-cheap` | bulk non-sensitive work, big context | `ask-cloud` → Gemini directly | live |
| `local-embed` | — | — | removed 2026-11-01, nothing called it (ADR 0018) |
| `local-code`, `local-chat` | — | alias names only; mean `local-big` (D1) | collapsed |
| `cloud-code`, `cloud-frontier*` | — | — | dormant, no keys |

## Enforcement

There is no gateway and no key scope (ADR 0016). The boundary is structural
in a different way:

| Path | Can it leave the host? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Anything to `127.0.0.1:1234` | **no** | LM Studio holds only local models |
| Zed on this host | **no** model egress | no cloud provider configured (D3). Exception: its `fetch` / `search_web` tools, and auslaw in the Research profile |
| `ask`, `ask-*` except `ask-cloud` | **no** | point at `:1234` with the host token |
| `ask-cloud` | **yes** | the only alias with a provider key; it is explicit by name |
| Sandboxed agents | **no** | egress allowed to `127.0.0.1:1234` only |
| Open WebUI | model: **no**. Search query: **yes if enabled** | see below |

What is lost against the gateway: nothing refuses a cloud request you make
on purpose. Choosing `ask-cloud` on sensitive work is no longer stopped — it
is only made explicit.

**Never add a cloud model to LM Studio**, and never give a remote backend a
`lmstudio` row in `config/roles.tsv`. That single rule is what makes `:1234`
safe for anything.

## Web search punctures the boundary

Open WebUI can give local models web search via self-hosted SearXNG
(`scripts/searxng.sh`). The model, the conversation and your documents all stay
on this machine. **The search query does not.**

SearXNG stops Google, Bing and DuckDuckGo seeing your IP or building a profile —
it queries them on your behalf. That is a genuine improvement over searching
directly. It is not privacy. The search terms still leave.

| Safe with web search on | Never with web search on |
|---|---|
| "How does framework X handle refresh tokens?" | "Search for <person name> <address>" |
| "What changed in the 2026 API spec?" | Anything quoting a customer, party, or account figure |
| Framework and API questions | Anything you would not type into Google |

Web search is opt-in per conversation. Leave it off by default. A conversation
that started with a client document should never have it enabled — earlier
context can be pulled into a generated search query.

The same applies to Zed's Research profile: every auslaw tool sends its query
to AustLII.

## No fallbacks

There is no router, so nothing can quietly reroute a request. If `local-big`
is down, requests fail — that is the intended behaviour. The earlier rule
("local roles never fall back to cloud") existed because the inherited gateway
had `local → gemini` fallbacks; see `docs/HISTORY.md`.

## Break-glass

There is no second local server. If LM Studio is wedged: quit the app,
reopen it, then `lms server start --bind 127.0.0.1 -p 1234; scripts/lms-load.sh`.
Before changing anything, capture `lms log stream`, the footprint, and the
concurrency bench at the failing settings (plan, phase 6).
# Runbook

Start, stop, diagnose. The LM Studio app serving `127.0.0.1:1234`, ADR 0016
(D6: the app, not headless `llmster`, since 2026-10-31). `lms` lives at `~/.lmstudio/bin/lms` (installed with
`--no-modify-path`; the aliases call it by full path as `$HEARTH_LMS`).

## Layout of the running system

| Piece | Where | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| LM Studio app | `/Applications/LM Studio.app` | owns the daemon and `~/.lmstudio`. "Enable Local LLM Service (headless)" on. Never run llmster as well |
| Server | `127.0.0.1:1234` | `lms server start --bind 127.0.0.1 -p 1234` |
| Roles | `config/roles.tsv` → `scripts/lms-load.sh` | `local-big` 128K × 3 parallel |
| Token | `~/.config/hearth/env`, mode 600 | `LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN`, created in the app |
| Server settings | `~/.lmstudio/.internal/http-server-config.json` | edited through the app; auth/tokens are stored elsewhere |
| Remote Login (sshd) | `:22` | laptops' tunnel; their keys are `restrict`ed to `-L` `:1234`/`:3000`, with `-R` denied |
| Open WebUI | Podman, `127.0.0.1:3000` | `scripts/webui.sh` |
| SearXNG | Podman, `:8888` | `scripts/searxng.sh` |

Removed 2026-10-31: Ollama, LiteLLM, Postgres, Rapid-MLX, llmster and their
launchd agents. Ports 4000, 5433 and 11434 should be free.

## First-time setup

1. Read, then run, the installer: `curl -fsSL https://lmstudio.ai/install.sh
   -o install.sh && less install.sh && sh install.sh --no-modify-path`.
2. Runtimes: `lms runtime get mlx-llm -y` and `lms runtime get llama.cpp -y`
   (the embedding model is GGUF).
3. Weights: `lms get <hugging face URL> -y` for each row in `config/roles.tsv`
   (exact sources in `docs/MODELS.md`). A bare staff-pick name with `-y` can
   choose a different quantisation. If `lms get` dies with "WebSocket
   connection closed", download the file from Hugging Face into
   `~/.lmstudio/models/<publisher>/<repo>/` and check its sha256.
4. **Server settings, once, in the LM Studio app** — llmster has no CLI for
   auth or tokens (lmstudio-ai/lms#489). Mode switch at the bottom → Developer;
   then the **Developer tab** in the left sidebar (not Settings) → Server
   Settings:

   | Setting | Value |
   |---|---|
   | Port | 1234 |
   | Require Authentication | **on** |
   | Serve on Local Network | off — laptops use an SSH tunnel |
   | Enable CORS | off |
   | Allow per-request MCPs | off |
   | Allow calling servers from mcp.json | off |
   | Just-in-Time Model Loading | off |
   | Log sensitive data | off — it writes prompts to the server logs |

   Manage Tokens → Create Token `host`. Store it without echoing:
   ```bash
   mkdir -p ~/.config/hearth && umask 077 && \
     printf 'LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN=%s\n' "$(pbpaste)" >> ~/.config/hearth/env && \
     chmod 600 ~/.config/hearth/env && pbcopy </dev/null
   ```
   In Settings → Developer turn **"Enable Local LLM Service (headless)" on**.
   If llmster was installed, stop it first (`lms daemon down` while the app is
   closed) — the two cannot share `~/.lmstudio`.
5. Start and load (app open):
   ```bash
   lms server start --bind 127.0.0.1 -p 1234
   scripts/lms-load.sh
   ```
6. Prove auth holds — a positive control first:
   ```bash
   . ~/.config/hearth/env
   curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -H "Authorization: Bearer $LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN" http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1/models   # 200
   curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1/models                                                    # 401
   ```
7. `echo "source $PWD/scripts/aliases.zsh" >> ~/.zshrc`

## Surviving a reboot

`scripts/install-autostart.sh --apply` installs `com.hearth.boot`, a launchd
agent that runs `scripts/boot.sh` once at login:

1. opens LM Studio hidden (stops llmster first if it grabbed the daemon);
2. **restarts the server with an explicit `--bind 127.0.0.1`** — the app's
   saved settings were found at `0.0.0.0` + CORS on (2026-10-31), and
   `lms server start --bind` rewrites them;
3. checks a no-token request gets 401;
4. runs `scripts/lms-load.sh`;
5. starts the Podman VM, SearXNG and Open WebUI.

The plist sets `AbandonProcessGroup`: without it launchd kills every process
`boot.sh` leaves behind when it exits — which is the Podman VM, so WebUI and
SearXNG died minutes after login (seen and reproduced 2026-10-31).

Log: `~/Library/Logs/hearth-boot.log`. Check it with
`scripts/install-autostart.sh --check`. By hand: `scripts/boot.sh`.

### Sleep

A sleeping host means every laptop loses inference. These are printed, not
run, because silently changing when your machine sleeps is not something a
script should do:

```bash
sudo pmset -c sleep 0        # never sleep on AC
sudo pmset -c disksleep 0
sudo pmset -a womp 1         # wake on network access
sudo pmset -c displaysleep 10   # displays can still sleep, that is free
```

## Everyday

```bash
ai-status                      # one glance: app, server, bind, roles, containers, autostart
ai-health                      # scripts/healthcheck.sh — the real verdict (exit 0/1/2)
scripts/preflight.sh           # static checks: roles.tsv, rule 1, saved settings, secrets, drift
ai-loaded                      # lms ps — identifiers, context, parallel
scripts/lms-load.sh --check    # drift against roles.tsv; changes nothing
scripts/lms-load.sh            # fix drift
ai-restart                     # server stop + start on 127.0.0.1:1234
ai-log                         # lms log stream — live requests
lms server status
lms daemon status
```

Footprint of the model worker (the number that matters against the ~48 GB
GPU ceiling — RSS understates it):

```bash
for p in $(pgrep -f '\.lmstudio/|LM Studio'); do footprint -p $p | awk '/Footprint:/'; done
```

## Chat UI and visibility

```bash
scripts/webui.sh up            # local only — there is no cloud mode
scripts/webui.sh up --lan      # reachable from other machines (see below)
scripts/webui.sh down
```

The container reaches the host's `127.0.0.1:1234` via
`host.containers.internal` with "serve on local network" off. `webui.sh up`
refuses to start unless the host token gets a 200.

**The connection lives in WebUI's database.** Environment variables only seed
a fresh database, so after the cutover the saved OpenAI connection may still
point at `:4000`. If the model list is empty: Admin Panel → Settings →
Connections → OpenAI API, URL `http://host.containers.internal:1234/v1`, key
= the host token, and delete any `:4000` / `:11434` entry. Default and task
model are `local-big`.

### Using the chat UI from a laptop

Open WebUI is a **web app** — nothing to install on the laptop. **Preferred:
SSH tunnel**, which keeps the UI localhost-only:

```bash
ssh -N -L 3000:127.0.0.1:3000 USER@HOST     # then http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

**Alternative:** `scripts/webui.sh up --lan`, then `http://<LAN-IP>:3000`. It
has its own login, but it is plain HTTP and another authenticated surface.
Either way, **do not port-forward :3000 to the internet.**

### Do not enable tools in Open WebUI without thinking

Web search feeds arbitrary attacker-controlled page text straight into the
model's context. That is tolerable **only** because the chat model has no tools:
it can mislead you convincingly, but it cannot act.

Enabling tools, functions, or code execution turns this into the highest-risk
surface in the stack. Open WebUI stores these settings in its database, where
they override the environment and survive container recreation. Treat
enabling them as an ADR-worthy decision. See `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` §3.

### Web search: the env vars are not enough

Same database-wins trap. Enable it where it counts:

```
Admin Panel -> Settings -> Web Search -> Enable Web Search
  Engine:    searxng
  Query URL: http://host.containers.internal:8888/search?q=<query>
```

The toggle then appears per message. If it vanishes, sign out and back in.

## Running agents contained

```bash
scripts/sandboxed.sh aider              # confine to $PWD
scripts/sandboxed.sh --dir ~/dev/x aider
scripts/sandboxed.sh --print-profile    # audit the profile
scripts/sandbox-probe.sh                # re-verify the controls still hold
```

Inside a sandboxed session the agent can reach **only** `127.0.0.1:1234`.
`pip install`, `npm install`, `git push` and any `curl` fail by design.

**Re-run `sandbox-probe.sh` after every macOS update.** `sandbox-exec` is
deprecated and Apple can change or remove it without notice.

If an agent crashes on a permission error naming a path outside the repo, that
is the sandbox working. Add a preset in `sandboxed.sh` or widen once with
`--also-write PATH`; every widening is printed at startup.

It does **not** protect against an agent reading your keys or corrupting the
repo it was invited into. Commit first; `git diff` before accepting.

## Diagnosing

| Symptom | Check | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Every client 401s | `curl` with the host token | token wrong or missing in `~/.config/hearth/env`; Zed keeps its own copy in the keychain |
| Wrong model answers | `scripts/lms-load.sh --check` | LM Studio answers an **unknown model name with the loaded LLM** instead of refusing (JIT off; nothing loads). Check the client's model id is a role |
| `lms daemon status` says "llmster … is running" | llmster took the daemon | `lms daemon down`, then open the app |
| Stuck "Local inference is changing ownership" | app and llmster both installed (lmstudio-bug-tracker#2485) | quit both; `lms daemon down; lms daemon up` |
| Slow under load | `concurrency-bench.sh` at the failing settings | prefill is serialised at any `--parallel`; see `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` |
| Zed agent stalls | `scripts/diagnose.sh`, `ai-log`, bench | capture all three **before** changing anything |
| Server LAN-visible or CORS on | `ai-status`, `ai-health` | `scripts/boot.sh` restarts it on 127.0.0.1 without CORS |
| aider errors naming `ollama_chat/…` or `local-code` | an old `.aider.conf.yml` in the repo dir | roles are `openai/local-big` now |

Bench any change the same way as the baseline:

```bash
scripts/concurrency-bench.sh --base-url http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1 \
  --token-env LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN --model local-big --concurrency 3 \
  --proc-match '(\.lmstudio/|LM Studio)'
```

## Rollback

There is no old stack to roll back to: it was removed on 2026-10-31 (ADR
0016, Rollback). Backups are in `~/hearth-backups/` (mode 700). Client config
backups end in `.pre-lmstudio-2026-10-31`.

## Laptop access

SSH tunnel only; the server stays on `127.0.0.1`. `docs/LAPTOP.md` has the
whole setup. On the host:

```bash
scripts/client-setup.sh <laptop-name>                       # print the steps
scripts/client-setup.sh <laptop-name> --authorize key.pub   # tunnel-only key
```

`healthcheck.sh` section 5 should say "LAN: no answer" — a LAN answer means
someone turned on Serve on Local Network. Revoke a laptop: delete its token in
LM Studio and its `hearth-tunnel-<name>` line in `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`.
**Never port-forward `:22`, `:1234` or `:3000`.**
# hearth — what it solves, what it measured, what's next

> **Historical (gateway era).** Describes the LiteLLM + Ollama stack; superseded by ADR 0016.

Written 2026-08-02. All figures below are measured on the machine, not estimated.

---

## 1. The problems this actually solves

### Privacy that survives forgetting

The real risk was never "I might paste client data into ChatGPT." It was that
in a hurry, on a deadline, the fast option and the safe option are different
buttons — and eventually the wrong one gets pressed.

So the boundary is **structural, not advisory**. Client work uses a `private`
API key whose model list contains no cloud models. Ask it for Gemini and the
gateway returns 403. There is no prompt that talks its way around a key scope.

The inherited config had a fallback rule sending `local → gemini` on any Ollama
timeout. Every slow local response was silently shipping the prompt to Google.
Nothing in the setup would ever have told me. **Across a privacy boundary a
fallback is a leak, not resilience** — `audit-fallbacks.sh` now fails the build
if that pattern reappears.

### One place to change models instead of four

Before: aider pointed at Ollama, Cursor at cloud providers, scripts elsewhere.
Changing model meant editing four things and missing one.

Now everything asks for a **role** — `local-chat`, `local-code`, `local-big`,
`local-embed`, `cloud-cheap`. Concrete model names live in exactly two files.
Swapping a model is one config edit, no client changes. Given how fast this
field moves, that indirection is the single most valuable design choice here.

### Knowing where requests went, and what they cost

`usage.sh --summary` gives the LOCAL vs CLOUD token split — the proportion of
work that never left the machine. Virtual keys carry spend caps that act as
fuses: a leaked key or a runaway overnight loop hits a wall instead of a card.

### Containment for agents (added today)

Coding agents run with full user permissions and read whatever you point them
at. The gateway governs where data goes *on purpose*; it has no jurisdiction
over a compromised agent, which would just call `curl` directly and never touch
LiteLLM at all.

Current state of the field, 2026: prompt injection **cannot be reliably
detected** — adaptive attacks bypass essentially every published defence, and
the best of them still miss around one in ten. OWASP has it as the leading
agentic-AI failure in production, with coding agents driving most of the volume.

So there is nothing to filter. The only thing that works is cutting capability.
`scripts/sandboxed.sh` runs an agent with egress denied to everything except
the gateway. It read a secret? Fine — there's no channel to send it down.

Verified on macOS 26.4, 10 checks passed. It proved itself within seconds of
first use: aider's startup call to pypi.org was refused by the sandbox.

**Deliberately not solved:** reads stay unrestricted (restricting them breaks
every language runtime), and an agent can still corrupt the repo it was invited
into. That second one is handled by committing before a session and reading
`git diff` after — a workflow habit, not a control.

---

## 2. Routerly comparison

[github.com/Inebrio/Routerly](https://github.com/Inebrio/Routerly), AGPL-3.0.
It targets the exact thing that is most annoying about the current setup.

| | Routerly | LiteLLM (what I run) |
|---|---|---|
| Database | **none** — JSON files | Postgres + Prisma, 64 tables |
| Native Anthropic `/v1/messages` | **yes** | no — OpenAI shape only |
| Per-project tokens + budgets | yes | yes (virtual keys) |
| Routing policies | 9, incl. LLM-picks-the-model | static fallbacks |
| Providers | 8 | 100+ |
| Maturity | v0.1.5 · 43 stars · 3 contributors | widely deployed |

**The genuine unlock is native Anthropic format.** Claude Code is currently a
documented exception in my stack because LiteLLM only speaks OpenAI shape.
Routerly handles `/v1/messages` natively, so Claude Code could route through
the gateway and pick up spend tracking and logging. That is a real capability
gain, not a tidiness argument.

**Why I am not switching:**

- v0.1.5, one release, three contributors. This is pre-1.0 software that would
  hold my provider keys and enforce my privacy boundary. LiteLLM's 64 tables
  are ugly; **ugly is not the same as risky.**
- The cost of switching is the *verification*, not the config. Leak test,
  fallback audit, digest pinning, every script touching `/key/*` — all would
  need redoing and re-proving. That is the expensive part, and it buys removing
  a container I never look at.
- AGPL plus a commercial-licensing contact is an open-core shape. Worth
  watching whether the free tier narrows.

Revisit at 1.0, or the first time Postgres causes an actual problem rather than
merely offending my sense of proportion.

---

## 3. Token speed on a 64 GB Mac — the counter-intuitive part

### Throughput is not the number that matters

Benched three models across four fixed tasks, through the gateway:

| Model | tokens emitted | wall clock | avg tok/s |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | 1,218 | 11.2 s | 108.8 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | 4,466 (3.7x) | 47.6 s | 93.8 |
| `qwen3:8b` (thinking on) | 13,840 (11.4x) | 181.4 s | 76.3 |

Throughput barely moves — **65–120 tok/s across everything**, because the
machine is memory-bandwidth bound. The 8B model is not meaningfully faster per
token than the 30B one.

**Wall clock is set by how many tokens a model emits, not how fast it emits
them.** The smallest model was the slowest overall, by 16x, because it is a
hybrid reasoning model that thinks out loud by default. On one task it spent
**100 seconds and 7,548 tokens** where the coding model took 1.6 s and 192
tokens — forty times the output for the same question.

Fix: Ollama's `think: false` parameter. `local-chat` went from **2,370 tokens /
29.7 s to 386 tokens / 5.1 s** through the gateway. From slowest role to
fastest.

Worth noting I got this wrong twice and measurement corrected me both times. I
doubted `think: false` would work — it did. I was confident `/no_think` in the
prompt would work — it was silently ignored, 2,236 tokens before and 2,261
after.

### Residency beats model quality

The old `local-big` was llama3.3:70b at 42 GB. Cold, through the gateway:

| Role | Model | Time to first reply |
|---|---|---|
| `local-code` | qwen2.5-coder:32b | 29 s |
| `local-big` | **llama3.3:70b** | **440 s** |
| `cloud-cheap` | gemini-3.5-flash | 2 s |

Seven and a half minutes. Not because the model is slow — because at 42 GB it
could not stay resident alongside anything else, so every switch meant
reloading 42 GB from disk. Replaced with `gpt-oss:20b`: **440 s → 5 s.** Not a
faster model, a resident one.

### The real memory ceiling is not 64 GB

macOS caps GPU-accessible unified memory at ~75% above 36 GB — so **~48 GB on a
64 GB machine**, not 64. I had ~54 GB written in the docs and it was wrong.

| | Old set | Current set |
|---|---|---|
| Weights resident | 65.7 GB (impossible) | **37.5 GB** |
| Against ceiling | ~48 GB | ~48 GB |
| Disk | 63 GB | ~37 GB |

Current: `qwen3:8b` 5.2 GB + `qwen3-coder:30b` 19 GB + `gpt-oss:20b` 13 GB +
`nomic-embed-text` 0.3 GB. All four stay loaded; switching roles evicts nothing.

**MoE models are what make this work.** `qwen3-coder:30b` is 30B parameters but
activates ~3B per token, so it runs at roughly 7B speed while fitting in 19 GB.

### On switching to llama-server

Looked at it after seeing Vitalik Buterin's writeup. **Expected saving: about
zero.** His gain came from a hard VRAM wall on a discrete 24 GB GPU, where a
model either fits or collapses to CPU offload. Unified memory has no such
cliff, and Ollama on Apple Silicon now runs on MLX — Apple's own framework for
this architecture. Cost would be ~15 scripts and ~60 call sites, to probably go
slower. Not doing it.

---

## 4. What's left

1. **Audit untrusted MCP servers** — the one remaining unknown. Fetches from the
   open web, sits next to real client data, nobody has asked what tools it hands
   a model.
2. **Re-bench with thinking off** and actually *read* the task 03 output.
   `gpt-oss:20b` earned `local-big` on capacity, not quality — nothing has yet
   confirmed it reasons well. Task 03 hides a float-for-currency bug in
   money arithmetic. A syntax check cannot answer that; it needs a human.
3. **Record model digests** from a verified state, so drift is detectable.
4. **Local reference corpus** via `local-embed`, so the queries most worth
   not leaking never reach a search engine at all.
5. **Confirmation gates** — costs nothing today because nothing in the stack can
   send mail or move money. Matters the day something can.

### Sharing the repo

Close. No secrets in any tracked file, and no hardcoded home paths in
executable code — 68 uses of `$HOME` instead. What remains is naming: a LAN IP
as a default in the client scripts, a personal launchd label, business-specific
rules in the agent instructions, and no LICENSE. Roughly half a day.

One item there is a real bug rather than cosmetic: `healthcheck.sh` probes a
hardcoded LAN address to confirm Ollama is *not* reachable. On any other
machine that address is unreachable anyway, so the check passes and reports the
hole closed on a box where it may be wide open. Same false-pass shape as the
leak test bug — unreachable and secure produce identical output.

---

## The pattern worth keeping

Nearly every real problem here was found by measurement, not reasoning:

- The fallback leak — found by reading config, not by it failing.
- `model_info: tier: local` — a valid-looking field that made LiteLLM silently
  drop **every model** while still passing its own readiness check.
- The leak test that passed against a dormant model, where "model not found"
  also returns 4xx. A broken key and a perfectly scoped key looked identical.
- The 70B — retired on a 440-second measurement, not on an opinion about 70B
  models.
- `/no_think` — confidently predicted to work, silently ignored.

The recurring shape: **a check that cannot run must never look like a check
that passed.** Preflight now separates ERROR (couldn't run) from FAIL (real
problem), and every security test pairs a positive control with the negative
one. That single distinction has caught more genuine problems than any
individual feature in the stack.
# Prompt injection threat model

Last updated: 2026-08-02. Controls in §4 verified on macOS 26.4 by
`scripts/sandbox-probe.sh` (10 passed, 0 failed).

## 1. The claim this document rests on

**Prompt injection cannot be detected reliably, and we do not try.**

As of 2026 the strongest published defences still miss roughly one in ten
optimisation-based attacks, and adaptive attacks bypass essentially every
recommended defence. OWASP's 2026 data has prompt injection as the leading
agentic-AI security failure in production, with coding agents driving most of
the new attack volume — **aider is named among the five agents responsible**,
and it is the one used daily here.

The consequence for design: there is no filter to install, no delimiter scheme
to adopt, and no classifier worth running. The only defences that hold are
**capability restrictions** — reducing what an agent is able to do, so that a
successful injection has nowhere to go.

Anything in this repo that looks like injection *detection* is a bug.

## 2. Why the existing privacy boundary does not help

This is the important structural point, and it is easy to get wrong.

Model placement is the control for **where data goes on purpose**. LM Studio
on `:1234` holds only local models, and only `ask-cloud` carries a cloud key, so
a prompt sent to a local role cannot route client data to Google. That boundary
is real and it works (ADR 0016; before that it was gateway key scope).

Injection attacks do not use that path at all.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph inputs["Untrusted inputs into the agent"]
        web["web pages via SearXNG"]
        repo["repo files / vendored deps"]
        mcpsrc["untrusted MCP - UNAUDITED"]
    end

    subgraph sandbox["sandbox-exec - one egress allowed"]
        agent["aider / Zed agent"]
        wd["working dir - writable"]
    end

    secrets["~/.ssh, .env, keychain<br/>readable - accepted"]

    gw{{"llmster 127.0.0.1:1234<br/>local models only, token required"}}
    localm["local models"]
    cloudm["cloud models"]
    outside["blocked: github / pypi / attacker"]

    web --> agent
    repo --> agent
    mcpsrc --> agent
    secrets -.-> agent

    agent -->|"only permitted egress"| gw
    agent --> wd
    agent -.->|"blocked by sandbox"| outside

    gw --> localm
    cloudm -.-|"not loaded in LM Studio"| gw

    classDef okCls fill:#E1F5EE,stroke:#0F6E56,color:#085041
    classDef badCls fill:#FBE9E7,stroke:#A32B18,color:#7A1F11
    classDef warnCls fill:#FAEEDA,stroke:#854F0B,color:#633806
    classDef boxCls fill:#EEEDFE,stroke:#534AB7,color:#3C3489

    class gw,localm okCls
    class outside badCls
    class secrets,mcpsrc warnCls
    class agent,wd boxCls
```

Two boundaries, doing different jobs:

- **`sandbox-exec`** wraps the agent. One arrow leaves it, to `127.0.0.1:1234`.
  Everything else — `github`, `pypi`, an attacker's host, DNS itself — is cut.
- **LM Studio** is the only thing at the end of that arrow, and it holds only
  local models, so even intended traffic cannot reach a cloud model.

An injection that lands still reads whatever it likes (the dashed arrow from
secrets is deliberate) and can still write into the working directory. What it
cannot do is get anything out.

An injected agent that reads `~/.ssh/id_rsa` and POSTs it to an attacker
**never touches the model server**. Which models are loaded has no bearing on
that path.

So: the privacy boundary governs *intentional* routing. The containment
boundary in §4 governs *everything else*. They are different axes and neither
substitutes for the other.

| Threat | Model placement | Sandbox |
|---|---|---|
| A prompt sends client data to a cloud model | **caught** (unless you run `ask-cloud`) | not caught |
| An injected agent exfiltrates a key via curl | not caught | **caught** |
| An injected agent reads a file it shouldn't | not caught | not caught |
| An injected agent rewrites source in the repo | not caught | not caught |

Note the bottom two rows. They are not oversights; see §5.

## 3. Surfaces, ranked by capability rather than likelihood

Ranking by likelihood invites guessing. Rank by *what an injection could do if
it landed*, which is knowable.

| # | Surface | Untrusted content arrives via | If injected, it can | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **aider** | files you `/add`, vendored deps, cloned repos, sample data | edit source, run commands, reach the network | **high** |
| 2 | **Zed agents** | same, plus anything the agent opens itself | same | **high** |
| 3 | **untrusted MCP** | fetched docs / tools from an unaudited server | depends entirely on what the MCP exposes — **unaudited** | unknown |
| 4 | **SearXNG → Open WebUI** | arbitrary web pages, per message | produce text. No tools are enabled, so it can mislead you but not act | low today |

**Surface 3 is the one with no number next to it.** It can sit next to real
  client data, it fetches from the open web, and nobody has asked what tools it
  hands a model. That is the next thing to audit.

**Surface 4's low rating is conditional and fragile.** The Open WebUI model is
inert because it has no tools — it can lie to you convincingly, which matters
for legal research, but it cannot act. **The moment tools, functions, or code
execution are enabled in Open WebUI, this becomes surface 1 with a web feed
attached.** Treat enabling them as a decision requiring an ADR, not a settings
toggle.

## 4. What is actually enforced

`scripts/sandboxed.sh` runs an agent under a `sandbox-exec` profile. Verified
on macOS 26.4, 2026-08-02:

| Control | State |
|---|---|
| Egress to anything but `127.0.0.1:1234` | **blocked** — verified against 1.1.1.1 and the old `:4000` (2026-10-31) |
| DNS resolution | **blocked** — hostnames do not resolve |
| Writes outside the working directory | **blocked** — verified against `$HOME` |
| Writes inside the working directory | allowed |
| Reads | **unrestricted, deliberately** |

Usage:

```bash
scripts/sandboxed.sh aider                 # confine to $PWD
scripts/sandboxed.sh --dir ~/dev/x aider   # confine to a named repo
scripts/sandboxed.sh --print-profile       # audit the profile, any machine
scripts/sandbox-probe.sh                   # re-verify the controls hold
```

Re-run the probe after any macOS update. `sandbox-exec` is deprecated and Apple
could remove or change it without notice; the probe is how you find out that
happened deliberately rather than by being breached.

### Why reads are open

Restricting reads breaks dylib loading and every language runtime, and read
confinement is not what stops the attack. An agent under this profile **can
still read your SSH keys, `.env` files, and browser cookies.** It cannot send
them anywhere. Exfiltration needs a channel, and there is exactly one open
channel — LM Studio on `:1234`, which holds only local models.

This is a deliberate trade, not a limitation we failed to notice. Anyone
reading this document should be able to state it back: *the sandbox protects
against exfiltration, not against reading.*

### Why the working directory is writable

An agent that cannot write is not an agent. The residual risk is that an
injected agent **corrupts the repo it was invited into** — plausible, and not
mitigated by the sandbox. It is mitigated by git: commit before starting an
agent session, and `git diff` before accepting anything. That is a workflow
control and it depends on you, which is why it is written down here rather than
assumed.

### What the sandbox is not

`sandbox-exec` is deprecated, has known weaknesses, and this profile allows
broad `mach-lookup` and `process-exec` because nothing runs without them. A
determined attacker with code execution may find leverage there.

This is **not** a VM and not a security boundary of that grade. It raises the
cost of an attack and closes the one channel that turns a compromise into a
loss. Do not extend trust to it beyond that. If the threat model ever includes
a motivated attacker specifically targeting this machine, the answer is a Lima
VM or a separate user account, not a better SBPL profile.

## 5. Accepted, unmitigated risk

Recorded so nobody mistakes silence for safety.

1. **An injected agent can read any file the user can.** Mitigated only by the
   absence of an egress channel. If a future change opens egress — even to a
   package registry — this becomes live. That is why `--also-write` and any
   network widening need justification.
2. **An injected agent can corrupt its own repo.** Mitigated by git hygiene,
   not by the sandbox.
3. **Untrusted MCP servers are unaudited.** Unknown capability, real-data adjacency.
4. **No confirmation gates** beyond git commits. This costs nothing today
   because nothing in the stack can send mail or move money. It becomes urgent
   the day something can.

## 6. Cheapest next checks

In order of value per effort:

1. Audit what tools each MCP exposes to a model. Surface 3 is the only
   unknown on the list.
2. Run aider under the sandbox for a week of real work and record what it
   needed widened. A profile nobody can work under gets switched off.
3. Confirm Open WebUI still has tools disabled after any upgrade — its settings
   live in a database that overrides the environment, a trap already documented
   in `RUNBOOK.md`.

## Sources

- [Prompt injection still drives most agentic AI security failures in production — Help Net Security / OWASP, June 2026](https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2026/06/11/owasp-prompt-injection-ai-security-failures/)
- [Adaptive Evaluation of Out-of-Band Defenses Against Prompt Injection in LLM Agents (arXiv)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.26479)
- [Prompt Injection Prevention for Coding Agents: Defenses That Hold in Production — Fiddler AI](https://www.fiddler.ai/blog/prompt-injection-prevention-coding-agents)
- [Clarify `sandbox-exec` deprecation timeline — apple/containerization issue #737](https://github.com/apple/containerization/issues/737)
- [Sandboxing Claude Code on macOS: What I Actually Found — Infralovers, Feb 2026](https://www.infralovers.com/blog/2026-02-15-sandboxing-claude-code-macos/)

---


You will be given a long file. Answer only from the file — do not use general
knowledge, and say "not in the file" if the answer is not there.

Questions:
1. What is the fallback chain for `cloud-frontier`?
2. Which roles are dormant, and what is each blocked on?
3. What port is Postgres bound to, and why that port specifically?

This task exists to separate models that genuinely use long context from ones
that skim the start and confabulate the rest. Run it with the repo's own docs
concatenated as input — see scripts/bench.sh.
